WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.504 --> 00:00:07.090
(mellow music)

00:01:10.445 --> 00:01:13.656
(people chatting)
(instruments tuning)

00:01:13.656 --> 00:01:15.784
- [Yuel] Should we just
double-check our (mumbles)?

00:01:15.784 --> 00:01:18.870
(instruments tuning)

00:01:27.337 --> 00:01:30.006
(chamber music)

00:01:37.305 --> 00:01:39.682
- I think there's a lot of
things going on that are

00:01:39.682 --> 00:01:42.977
driving people to
experiment, and to explore,

00:01:42.977 --> 00:01:45.313
and to hunger for something different.

00:01:45.313 --> 00:01:46.981
It's a very interesting time in history

00:01:46.981 --> 00:01:48.483
where people are beginning to see

00:01:48.483 --> 00:01:51.402
the old system isn't working.

00:01:51.402 --> 00:01:55.573
Not only economically,
ecologically, but in human terms.

00:01:59.285 --> 00:02:01.287
- You can look at cooperation

00:02:01.287 --> 00:02:04.249
as an ethical, good-hearted thing.

00:02:04.249 --> 00:02:08.169
Or you can look at it as
something people need to do

00:02:08.169 --> 00:02:10.004
to take their work seriously,

00:02:10.004 --> 00:02:11.673
to take other people seriously,

00:02:11.673 --> 00:02:14.425
to organize politically.

00:02:14.425 --> 00:02:16.678
This isn't touchy feely stuff,

00:02:16.678 --> 00:02:20.765
this is about actually
getting the world to work.

00:02:22.016 --> 00:02:23.476
- We're in transition,

00:02:23.476 --> 00:02:26.604
and what's remarkable to
me is that people feel that

00:02:26.604 --> 00:02:27.856
in their bones.

00:02:27.856 --> 00:02:29.399
We need to figure out how to align

00:02:29.399 --> 00:02:31.359
the human economy with the
principles and patterns

00:02:31.359 --> 00:02:34.279
that tend to operate
in all living systems.

00:02:34.279 --> 00:02:35.864
That's the essence of it.

00:02:35.864 --> 00:02:37.740
And so, the challenge is to kind of

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:39.742
figure out how to switch
out the engines on the plane

00:02:39.742 --> 00:02:41.619
while we're keeping the plane in the air.

00:02:41.619 --> 00:02:44.289
(chamber music)

00:03:09.606 --> 00:03:11.858
- What if we were able to

00:03:11.858 --> 00:03:15.778
really build the foundations
of a cooperative world?

00:03:15.778 --> 00:03:17.614
What would it look like?

00:03:17.614 --> 00:03:19.949
What would be free and exciting?

00:03:19.949 --> 00:03:21.743
What would be generous?

00:03:21.743 --> 00:03:23.411
What would be moral?

00:03:24.579 --> 00:03:28.875
What would be exciting?
What would be liberating?

00:03:28.875 --> 00:03:31.961
That's the kind of direction
we're talking about.

00:03:31.961 --> 00:03:34.547
We're at ground zero beginning to explore

00:03:34.547 --> 00:03:36.549
designs that take us beyond the

00:03:36.549 --> 00:03:40.178
20th century capitalism,
20th century socialism.

00:03:40.178 --> 00:03:42.263
There are a lot of people beginning to say

00:03:42.263 --> 00:03:43.848
there's gotta be a better way.

00:03:43.848 --> 00:03:45.892
You talking about changing the system,

00:03:45.892 --> 00:03:47.602
the whole system?

00:03:47.602 --> 00:03:49.604
That's what this is about,

00:03:49.604 --> 00:03:51.731
but that means people have to really

00:03:51.731 --> 00:03:54.150
start small but also, think big.

00:03:54.150 --> 00:03:56.819
(mellow music)

00:04:12.126 --> 00:04:15.421
- Housing is one of the
primary social institutions.

00:04:15.421 --> 00:04:19.300
We have an opportunity
through technology and

00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:21.469
kind of open collaborative

00:04:22.512 --> 00:04:24.973
philosophies that have been
emerging in the last few years

00:04:24.973 --> 00:04:27.976
to really rethink the way that
the built environment works

00:04:27.976 --> 00:04:32.355
and the way that major
societal structures work.

00:04:32.355 --> 00:04:34.482
I have to instigate the project

00:04:34.482 --> 00:04:36.150
that we're sitting in right now.

00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:37.694
It's called the Red Victorian,

00:04:37.694 --> 00:04:39.862
and it's a 20-room hotel,

00:04:39.862 --> 00:04:42.949
and it's a bed and breakfast,
it's been here for 100 years.

00:04:42.949 --> 00:04:45.034
We took it over about a year ago,

00:04:45.034 --> 00:04:49.205
and turned it into what we're
calling a co-living hotel.

00:04:50.498 --> 00:04:53.042
The Red Victorian is really

00:04:53.042 --> 00:04:55.420
a new way of thinking
about home and housing

00:04:55.420 --> 00:04:57.088
in the 21st century.

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:00.508
It's a combination of

00:05:00.508 --> 00:05:02.552
long term residential accommodations but

00:05:02.552 --> 00:05:06.723
in a very fluid way with
short term housing options.

00:05:06.723 --> 00:05:09.225
So, we have people staying
here everywhere from one night

00:05:09.225 --> 00:05:12.145
to one month to indefinitely.

00:05:12.145 --> 00:05:14.188
We're aiming to serve the kind of modern,

00:05:14.188 --> 00:05:15.982
nomadic creative population

00:05:15.982 --> 00:05:17.483
and so, people are often traveling,

00:05:17.483 --> 00:05:18.318
or they're working late,

00:05:18.318 --> 00:05:21.821
or their schedules are very different.

00:05:21.821 --> 00:05:24.282
We don't have a particular hierarchy,

00:05:24.282 --> 00:05:27.368
and I certainly wouldn't call myself

00:05:27.368 --> 00:05:29.704
the boss or the manager,
or we're all managers,

00:05:29.704 --> 00:05:32.206
or you can think of it
in many different ways,

00:05:32.206 --> 00:05:35.835
but it's a cooperatively run space.

00:05:35.835 --> 00:05:37.503
- [Man] Okay, which one are we looking at?

00:05:37.503 --> 00:05:38.338
- [Woman] Can I show you something?

00:05:38.338 --> 00:05:39.422
- [Man] Sure.

00:05:39.422 --> 00:05:42.342
- The way we make decisions
at the Red Victorian is

00:05:42.342 --> 00:05:45.762
open to everybody who's in the community.

00:05:45.762 --> 00:05:48.222
I went and I met with the
Department of Public Health,

00:05:48.222 --> 00:05:50.475
and they provided a couple of options for

00:05:50.475 --> 00:05:52.977
what we could do in the
short term to be compliant

00:05:52.977 --> 00:05:55.146
with what health codes are required.

00:05:55.146 --> 00:05:57.857
So, the first is that
we could be officially

00:05:57.857 --> 00:05:58.900
a bed and breakfast,

00:05:58.900 --> 00:06:00.360
providing the breakfast

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:02.070
and other things that go along with that.

00:06:02.070 --> 00:06:04.614
It means that only
employees and innkeepers,

00:06:04.614 --> 00:06:07.408
which they don't even know
what they're defined as,

00:06:07.408 --> 00:06:08.743
may provide food for the guests,

00:06:08.743 --> 00:06:09.786
but we have to provide it,

00:06:09.786 --> 00:06:10.745
and the guests can't provide it,

00:06:10.745 --> 00:06:12.330
can't access it themselves.

00:06:12.330 --> 00:06:13.748
- That's so, silly 'cause it like

00:06:13.748 --> 00:06:17.460
precludes you from doing
new and innovative stuff.

00:06:17.460 --> 00:06:19.128
- We basically don't fit
into any of the boxes

00:06:19.128 --> 00:06:21.381
that they have for anything.

00:06:21.381 --> 00:06:22.298
They could basically say

00:06:22.298 --> 00:06:23.508
you've gotta shut down your hotel

00:06:23.508 --> 00:06:26.094
because you're not complaint.

00:06:26.094 --> 00:06:27.970
- We're witnessing an era where

00:06:27.970 --> 00:06:30.181
the diversity of economies
are starting to flourish

00:06:30.181 --> 00:06:32.433
and so, government
doesn't know what to do.

00:06:32.433 --> 00:06:34.685
- But this seems to be
written for like a B&amp;B

00:06:34.685 --> 00:06:36.854
that's family run.

00:06:36.854 --> 00:06:38.356
- Yeah, yeah.
- Is what I'm seeing here.

00:06:38.356 --> 00:06:39.482
- Family run bed and breakfast,

00:06:39.482 --> 00:06:40.483
that's a thing right?
- Yeah.

00:06:40.483 --> 00:06:42.151
- But yeah that's, that's actually...

00:06:42.151 --> 00:06:43.820
It turns out a co-living
hotel is a family run

00:06:43.820 --> 00:06:44.946
bed and breakfast.

00:06:44.946 --> 00:06:46.280
Who knew?
- Easy.

00:06:47.407 --> 00:06:49.659
- Thanks everyone for coming tonight.

00:06:49.659 --> 00:06:53.121
This is our Wednesday
dinner at the Red Victorian.

00:06:53.121 --> 00:06:56.624
For those of you who may
not have been here before,

00:06:56.624 --> 00:06:58.626
the Red Vic is our home.

00:06:58.626 --> 00:07:01.337
(people chatting)

00:07:01.337 --> 00:07:03.047
- [Jessy Kate] I'm
really inspired by living

00:07:03.047 --> 00:07:04.966
around and being exposed to people

00:07:04.966 --> 00:07:07.051
who are up to amazing things in the world.

00:07:07.051 --> 00:07:10.221
We can provide an
environment and a structure

00:07:10.221 --> 00:07:14.642
that not only selfishly gets
us exposed to those people,

00:07:14.642 --> 00:07:15.476
but actually,

00:07:15.476 --> 00:07:17.645
I think supports and amplifies
the work that they're doing

00:07:17.645 --> 00:07:20.565
by making it easier for them
to do what they're doing

00:07:20.565 --> 00:07:22.775
and also, to hopefully meet others

00:07:22.775 --> 00:07:25.069
who are inspiring to them.

00:07:25.069 --> 00:07:27.780
- We've been totally
bootstrapped up to this point.

00:07:27.780 --> 00:07:29.657
- I mean I think that this

00:07:29.657 --> 00:07:31.117
to me

00:07:31.117 --> 00:07:34.495
a very exciting aspect of
having a physical building,

00:07:34.495 --> 00:07:37.039
is that it's an opportunity to provide

00:07:37.039 --> 00:07:39.667
literally a container for
a whole bunch of humans

00:07:39.667 --> 00:07:40.835
to walk through the door,

00:07:40.835 --> 00:07:42.170
and when you walk through that door,

00:07:42.170 --> 00:07:45.214
we get to say hey the rules are
a little bit different here.

00:07:45.214 --> 00:07:47.175
We really like to play
with that in terms of

00:07:47.175 --> 00:07:48.426
having people getting to say

00:07:48.426 --> 00:07:50.219
actually here you do have a say in things,

00:07:50.219 --> 00:07:51.637
and then looking at it and saying,

00:07:51.637 --> 00:07:52.889
okay well how's that working?

00:07:52.889 --> 00:07:54.432
Actually that didn't work very well

00:07:54.432 --> 00:07:55.475
but that worked really well,

00:07:55.475 --> 00:07:56.934
okay let's update how we're doing things

00:07:56.934 --> 00:08:00.646
and try it again and instead
of it's a big experiment.

00:08:00.646 --> 00:08:03.316
(chamber music)

00:08:21.250 --> 00:08:24.462
- I first really understood
about cooperation

00:08:24.462 --> 00:08:27.632
because my background is as a musician

00:08:27.632 --> 00:08:30.218
and of course when we play chamber music

00:08:30.218 --> 00:08:34.847
or any kind of music that
doesn't have a conductor,

00:08:34.847 --> 00:08:37.683
we have to find ways to cooperate.

00:08:37.683 --> 00:08:40.102
You know the way most musicians rehearse

00:08:40.102 --> 00:08:41.103
is by grunts

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:44.649
or eye contact you know or

00:08:45.650 --> 00:08:46.734
grimaces.

00:08:46.734 --> 00:08:49.403
(chamber music}

00:08:54.659 --> 00:08:58.871
- Like being able to really
listen to the other person,

00:08:58.871 --> 00:09:01.832
all three of them in fact,
you're kind of trying to hear

00:09:01.832 --> 00:09:04.085
three voices in your head at the same time

00:09:04.085 --> 00:09:06.504
as you're looking at your own
music and playing together so,

00:09:06.504 --> 00:09:10.591
you kind of get into the flow as a group.

00:09:10.591 --> 00:09:12.552
Do you know what, I think we
need to practice number 1,

00:09:12.552 --> 00:09:14.387
just right from there, from 1 to 2.

00:09:14.387 --> 00:09:15.972
Yeah, why don't we try one at a time.

00:09:15.972 --> 00:09:19.392
Why don't you do it once with her from 1?

00:09:21.602 --> 00:09:24.647
- Yeah, we are part of
one thing and you know

00:09:24.647 --> 00:09:26.816
each one of us have a role

00:09:27.650 --> 00:09:30.403
and sometimes we trade in roles.

00:09:30.403 --> 00:09:32.488
It's a real conversation,

00:09:33.406 --> 00:09:35.825
well, reminiscent of life.

00:09:35.825 --> 00:09:37.660
It's communicating.

00:09:37.660 --> 00:09:38.536
It's fun.

00:09:38.536 --> 00:09:41.205
(chamber music)

00:09:51.632 --> 00:09:55.469
- We are on about 1 1/2
acres of urban land.

00:09:55.469 --> 00:09:57.013
It was a parking lot

00:09:57.013 --> 00:10:00.474
and through a partnership
with Concord Pacific

00:10:00.474 --> 00:10:03.477
we have been able to farm this land.

00:10:06.230 --> 00:10:08.316
So, we are an urban farm.

00:10:12.945 --> 00:10:16.449
- We felt certainly a great
need on the downtown east side

00:10:16.449 --> 00:10:19.160
that nothing agricultural was going on,

00:10:19.160 --> 00:10:22.913
that a social enterprise
based on agriculture

00:10:22.913 --> 00:10:27.084
had the potential to not
only provide meaningful work,

00:10:27.084 --> 00:10:30.504
but also, a number of
other healing aspects

00:10:30.504 --> 00:10:32.882
that occur by nature of working the soil,

00:10:32.882 --> 00:10:35.176
growing living things,
taking care of something,

00:10:35.176 --> 00:10:36.927
feeding other people.

00:10:38.846 --> 00:10:42.141
So, we have five farms, each is different.

00:10:42.141 --> 00:10:44.560
They each are in different neighborhoods.

00:10:44.560 --> 00:10:46.520
They each are doing different things.

00:10:46.520 --> 00:10:49.357
This is an orchard for example.

00:10:49.357 --> 00:10:51.150
Pacific and Carol, our largest site,

00:10:51.150 --> 00:10:53.069
big production,

00:10:53.069 --> 00:10:55.780
and First and Clark an entire acre,

00:10:55.780 --> 00:10:57.281
16,000 square feet

00:10:58.491 --> 00:10:59.992
of unheated tunnel houses.

00:10:59.992 --> 00:11:02.244
You know where we're
producing tomatoes and peppers

00:11:02.244 --> 00:11:04.914
and melons and things like that.

00:11:06.374 --> 00:11:09.168
So, we have to, because of the,

00:11:09.168 --> 00:11:13.964
intensity and scale of the
operation, we have to make

00:11:13.964 --> 00:11:16.425
maximum use of time and space.

00:11:16.425 --> 00:11:18.928
So, as soon as a crop is done, boom,

00:11:18.928 --> 00:11:20.429
replant, you know.

00:11:22.264 --> 00:11:25.476
Here people using these
words urban agriculture.

00:11:25.476 --> 00:11:27.061
Nobody had ever heard
those two words together,

00:11:27.061 --> 00:11:29.355
when I would describe
it, it was like what?

00:11:29.355 --> 00:11:31.065
And we felt we needed to demonstrate

00:11:31.065 --> 00:11:35.820
that it was possible to
have a successful enterprise

00:11:35.820 --> 00:11:38.406
business based on agriculture in the city

00:11:38.406 --> 00:11:41.117
and at the same time support

00:11:41.117 --> 00:11:43.160
people's needs.

00:11:43.160 --> 00:11:44.704
We had to bring the farm to the city.

00:11:44.704 --> 00:11:47.915
Yeah, we had to create within these boxes

00:11:47.915 --> 00:11:50.000
an entire ecosystem.

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:51.127
Kinda crazy,

00:11:51.127 --> 00:11:52.294
imperfect,

00:11:52.294 --> 00:11:53.796
but has potential.

00:11:57.758 --> 00:11:59.844
- We are providing employment to people

00:11:59.844 --> 00:12:01.053
who are facing barriers.

00:12:01.053 --> 00:12:02.138
Sometimes that's an addiction,

00:12:02.138 --> 00:12:03.639
sometimes that's a mental illness,

00:12:03.639 --> 00:12:05.725
sometimes it's a physical illness.

00:12:05.725 --> 00:12:07.685
Whatever the barrier,

00:12:07.685 --> 00:12:09.895
it makes it hard for
most of our staff to work

00:12:09.895 --> 00:12:11.105
a regular job.

00:12:11.105 --> 00:12:13.816
They need a job where there's
flexibility and understanding

00:12:13.816 --> 00:12:17.027
about the ups and downs of their lives.

00:12:17.027 --> 00:12:19.447
- Right there, lemon tree

00:12:19.447 --> 00:12:20.448
with lemons,

00:12:22.908 --> 00:12:24.076
right?

00:12:24.076 --> 00:12:25.619
That's special.

00:12:25.619 --> 00:12:27.037
It's awesome yeah, yeah.

00:12:27.037 --> 00:12:29.206
I missed my calling, man.

00:12:29.206 --> 00:12:32.626
I should have been a fucking gardener,

00:12:32.626 --> 00:12:34.462
instead of what I was.

00:12:40.176 --> 00:12:41.552
It's not very often down here

00:12:41.552 --> 00:12:43.095
'cause you know you're
pretty rough around the edges

00:12:43.095 --> 00:12:47.016
that you get to care and
nurture for something.

00:12:53.939 --> 00:12:57.234
When I was a kid, punishment
was going to weed the garden.

00:12:57.234 --> 00:13:00.488
so, when I came here it's
like, oh man what am I doing.

00:13:00.488 --> 00:13:01.864
I remember this.

00:13:01.864 --> 00:13:03.449
But it's not like that.

00:13:03.449 --> 00:13:05.075
You get into the soil and stuff.

00:13:05.075 --> 00:13:07.077
You get to see stuff grow and, actually,

00:13:07.077 --> 00:13:08.996
it's kind of beautiful.

00:13:08.996 --> 00:13:13.167
It is beautiful, it's not kinda
beautiful, it is beautiful.

00:13:14.960 --> 00:13:17.671
- Sole Food has two primary goals.

00:13:17.671 --> 00:13:18.798
One is the a social one,

00:13:18.798 --> 00:13:21.217
and I think one is an agricultural one.

00:13:21.217 --> 00:13:23.677
The social one is first and foremost.

00:13:23.677 --> 00:13:24.970
The agricultural one

00:13:24.970 --> 00:13:27.932
dominates and then forms the social piece.

00:13:27.932 --> 00:13:29.725
This as a community,

00:13:29.725 --> 00:13:30.851
this is a place to go,

00:13:30.851 --> 00:13:32.853
this is a reason to get up in the morning.

00:13:32.853 --> 00:13:34.188
This has a choice between

00:13:34.188 --> 00:13:35.356
something that's gonna bring you down

00:13:35.356 --> 00:13:38.442
and something that might be positive.

00:13:39.985 --> 00:13:42.947
This is where you're getting
hung up on right here.

00:13:42.947 --> 00:13:44.490
- 'Cause these guys, they
don't give up on you.

00:13:44.490 --> 00:13:46.242
You don't show up, you're
not feeling too good,

00:13:46.242 --> 00:13:48.702
you're not making it,
come back the next day,

00:13:48.702 --> 00:13:49.954
we'll keep working.

00:13:49.954 --> 00:13:51.914
You don't get fired here.

00:13:51.914 --> 00:13:52.998
And hey, I come in every day.

00:13:52.998 --> 00:13:55.000
Oh, I missed one day last month

00:13:55.000 --> 00:13:58.170
and that's the only day I missed.

00:13:58.170 --> 00:14:01.257
I do all the seeding here just about.

00:14:03.133 --> 00:14:06.554
Yeah I guess I'm the boss for here.

00:14:06.554 --> 00:14:07.680
Tell people where they need to be,

00:14:07.680 --> 00:14:10.015
what needs to get done
and stuff like that,

00:14:10.015 --> 00:14:12.518
but it's only my second
year and I'm still learning,

00:14:12.518 --> 00:14:14.103
and I'm learning from the best.

00:14:14.103 --> 00:14:15.563
Lissa is a beast.

00:14:15.563 --> 00:14:17.773
She might be small, she's strong too.

00:14:17.773 --> 00:14:19.775
She can work her butt off.

00:14:19.775 --> 00:14:21.610
- [Michael] She's awesome.

00:14:21.610 --> 00:14:25.155
She really kinda holds
us all together for us.

00:14:25.155 --> 00:14:26.991
And she's a good weeder.

00:14:26.991 --> 00:14:27.825
- Thanks Michael.

00:14:27.825 --> 00:14:30.911
At least I know I can't mess that up.

00:14:33.956 --> 00:14:35.332
Why do I do this?

00:14:35.332 --> 00:14:39.503
Some days I don't know. (laughing)

00:14:40.337 --> 00:14:43.173
I mean we all want the
world to be a better place

00:14:43.173 --> 00:14:45.259
and I think that there's real,

00:14:45.259 --> 00:14:47.803
for me personally, there was
a lot of transformative power

00:14:47.803 --> 00:14:49.972
in agriculture in my life.

00:14:52.182 --> 00:14:55.144
I come from a really privileged world.

00:14:55.144 --> 00:14:56.812
I went to university

00:14:56.812 --> 00:14:59.189
which was paid for
primarily by my parents.

00:14:59.189 --> 00:15:00.983
I grew up in a stable household

00:15:00.983 --> 00:15:03.319
with a really loving family.

00:15:05.154 --> 00:15:09.700
I had people all along telling
me that I could do things.

00:15:09.700 --> 00:15:12.161
It is quite hard coming in this.

00:15:12.161 --> 00:15:15.748
You try to find a common
ground with people.

00:15:15.748 --> 00:15:18.167
I don't know what it's like to
go through opiate withdrawal.

00:15:18.167 --> 00:15:21.378
I don't know what it's
like not be able to get

00:15:21.378 --> 00:15:25.549
my prescription in the morning
when I need it every day.

00:15:26.383 --> 00:15:28.177
There's days when like I
really can't understand

00:15:28.177 --> 00:15:32.348
what someone's dealing with
but you can be compassionate.

00:15:32.348 --> 00:15:35.017
(mellow music)

00:15:41.106 --> 00:15:43.233
- A lot of people on the east side

00:15:43.233 --> 00:15:45.152
are great people,

00:15:45.152 --> 00:15:47.696
it's just that they don't
think that they're worth

00:15:47.696 --> 00:15:49.907
the puddle that they're stepping in,

00:15:49.907 --> 00:15:52.242
and that's the truth, right?

00:15:57.790 --> 00:16:01.919
I've been a heroin addict
for about 20 years.

00:16:01.919 --> 00:16:04.171
I battle with addiction and

00:16:09.301 --> 00:16:11.553
it didn't matter to them.

00:16:11.553 --> 00:16:14.056
Like it didn't matter to them.

00:16:15.224 --> 00:16:18.978
You know I had track
marks, it didn't matter.

00:16:20.521 --> 00:16:23.232
I wasn't being judged, you know, like

00:16:23.232 --> 00:16:25.484
what's going on here right?

00:16:27.236 --> 00:16:28.654
- [Michael] The power
of what we've done here

00:16:28.654 --> 00:16:30.280
has to be shared,

00:16:30.280 --> 00:16:31.198
both by the

00:16:32.199 --> 00:16:33.492
influence

00:16:33.492 --> 00:16:34.660
of the

00:16:34.660 --> 00:16:37.830
soil and the plants and the healthy food,

00:16:37.830 --> 00:16:40.040
but also, by the community of people who

00:16:40.040 --> 00:16:42.584
have been involved with this experience.

00:16:42.584 --> 00:16:44.420
It's about the people.

00:16:50.592 --> 00:16:52.594
- What we see emerging is what we call

00:16:52.594 --> 00:16:54.263
a regenerative economy,

00:16:54.263 --> 00:16:57.349
and in very simple terms,
we're having this conversation

00:16:57.349 --> 00:16:59.476
because our bodies have been,

00:16:59.476 --> 00:17:01.228
and are continuing to regenerate.

00:17:01.228 --> 00:17:04.273
So, we're sustainable
because we're regenerating,

00:17:04.273 --> 00:17:06.775
and the whole universe works that way.

00:17:06.775 --> 00:17:07.651
And we see it everywhere.

00:17:07.651 --> 00:17:10.654
And usually driven by the
intuition of an entrepreneur

00:17:10.654 --> 00:17:12.698
who has no theoretical framework in mind.

00:17:12.698 --> 00:17:13.615
They intuit it.

00:17:13.615 --> 00:17:16.201
So, that's why I'm confident
that it's an emergent property,

00:17:16.201 --> 00:17:17.244
and it's amazing

00:17:17.244 --> 00:17:21.540
how often we see these
patterns repeating themselves.

00:17:21.540 --> 00:17:24.209
(mellow music)

00:17:31.592 --> 00:17:32.885
- The London Brewing Cooperative

00:17:32.885 --> 00:17:36.430
is located in London's old east village.

00:17:36.430 --> 00:17:39.016
Tight community, vibrant community,

00:17:39.016 --> 00:17:42.686
a community that's really
has its own identity

00:17:42.686 --> 00:17:44.521
and is moving forward.

00:17:49.568 --> 00:17:51.987
- I suppose it's got its roots in

00:17:51.987 --> 00:17:56.784
my friend Marcus receiving
a used book for his birthday

00:17:56.784 --> 00:17:57.618
Papazian's

00:17:59.036 --> 00:18:00.829
New Complete Joy of Home Brewing

00:18:00.829 --> 00:18:03.123
and he sent me an email and
said hey you wanna brew beer?

00:18:03.123 --> 00:18:04.708
And I liked beer.

00:18:04.708 --> 00:18:07.294
I had no idea it could be made.

00:18:08.378 --> 00:18:11.548
- We were brewing together
you know in my garage

00:18:11.548 --> 00:18:14.176
just with you know something we built.

00:18:14.176 --> 00:18:16.970
It was a propane-fired,
gravity-fed system.

00:18:16.970 --> 00:18:19.056
I mean we didn't paint
it up even to look nice.

00:18:19.056 --> 00:18:20.516
It was very utilitarian,

00:18:20.516 --> 00:18:23.268
it made beer, it made very good beer.

00:18:23.268 --> 00:18:25.729
Everybody has a good
time brewing the beer.

00:18:25.729 --> 00:18:27.898
Everybody has a good time
sitting around and chatting.

00:18:27.898 --> 00:18:31.360
And usually beer brings
out some good debate,

00:18:31.360 --> 00:18:33.112
brings people together.

00:18:33.112 --> 00:18:35.614
- It was a very makeshift system

00:18:35.614 --> 00:18:38.075
that we had you know thrown together and

00:18:38.075 --> 00:18:41.078
garage door wide open so the
propane fumes could go out

00:18:41.078 --> 00:18:44.957
and you know we were just having
a lot of fun brewing beer.

00:18:44.957 --> 00:18:48.085
We started to realize
these recipes are actually

00:18:48.085 --> 00:18:50.504
people kinda like them
and it's starting to work.

00:18:50.504 --> 00:18:53.924
- And we looked at different models of

00:18:53.924 --> 00:18:55.801
cooperative brewing,

00:18:55.801 --> 00:18:58.512
ways that a brewery could both be

00:18:58.512 --> 00:19:02.683
financially viable but
also be collectively owned.

00:19:02.683 --> 00:19:04.476
We couldn't figure it out,

00:19:04.476 --> 00:19:06.228
so we just kept brewing,

00:19:06.228 --> 00:19:08.355
but we just enlarged the circle.

00:19:08.355 --> 00:19:11.108
At that point it was myself, Dave...

00:19:11.108 --> 00:19:12.484
Dave often jokes that he got involved

00:19:12.484 --> 00:19:14.069
'cause he had a garage.

00:19:14.069 --> 00:19:15.904
That's partially true.

00:19:18.323 --> 00:19:19.533
- [Gar] I think people want

00:19:19.533 --> 00:19:22.369
to be on big decisions that matter.

00:19:22.369 --> 00:19:25.247
How to structure that
becomes really tricky.

00:19:25.247 --> 00:19:29.418
We have to learn that, it
doesn't come naturally.

00:19:29.418 --> 00:19:31.962
A cooperative is simply
a one person, one vote

00:19:31.962 --> 00:19:33.463
ownership structure.

00:19:33.463 --> 00:19:35.799
So, you get a group of people together,

00:19:35.799 --> 00:19:36.967
everybody gets a vote,

00:19:36.967 --> 00:19:41.138
and you set up a company or
a bank or a store or a farm.

00:19:41.138 --> 00:19:43.557
It's a very simple structure.

00:19:46.643 --> 00:19:49.771
- We purposely located
ourselves in old east village.

00:19:49.771 --> 00:19:51.023
It's a neighborhood we love,

00:19:51.023 --> 00:19:53.233
it's a neighborhood
that many of us live in

00:19:53.233 --> 00:19:57.070
and so, we want to be
part of that community.

00:19:57.070 --> 00:19:59.615
So, we don't want to have a huge

00:19:59.615 --> 00:20:02.910
you know pollution
spewing kind of operation.

00:20:02.910 --> 00:20:05.954
We wanna have a community brewery

00:20:05.954 --> 00:20:09.833
so, one that everybody
feels they can be a part of,

00:20:09.833 --> 00:20:11.251
everyone feels that

00:20:11.251 --> 00:20:13.003
is doing things that
are in the best interest

00:20:13.003 --> 00:20:14.421
of the community.

00:20:17.299 --> 00:20:19.635
- Co-ops, I mean, they're
a very old business model,

00:20:19.635 --> 00:20:21.678
they're an underutilized, business model,

00:20:21.678 --> 00:20:24.264
and at the premise they're about a more,

00:20:24.264 --> 00:20:26.975
equitable distribution
of power and wealth.

00:20:26.975 --> 00:20:28.435
- If we can just make a couple of notes of

00:20:28.435 --> 00:20:31.897
positive and then things
to improve on maybe?

00:20:31.897 --> 00:20:33.565
- The idea is a very

00:20:34.483 --> 00:20:37.361
beautiful one when you
think about how it can work

00:20:37.361 --> 00:20:39.071
and how everyone works together.

00:20:39.071 --> 00:20:41.365
- Do it on a night that,

00:20:41.365 --> 00:20:44.243
like on a Friday or a
Saturday night potentially,

00:20:44.243 --> 00:20:46.370
when we're gonna be busy in the cellar.

00:20:46.370 --> 00:20:47.788
- It's one member one vote.

00:20:47.788 --> 00:20:49.039
It's not $1 one vote.

00:20:49.039 --> 00:20:51.667
So, it doesn't matter how
much of the company you own,

00:20:51.667 --> 00:20:53.043
how much you have invested,

00:20:53.043 --> 00:20:56.546
it's that you have a stake in the outcome.

00:20:57.673 --> 00:20:59.341
- That's a food house,
that's a real food house.

00:20:59.341 --> 00:21:01.551
- So, there's no need for a president,

00:21:01.551 --> 00:21:03.595
there's no need for a secretary,

00:21:03.595 --> 00:21:05.180
but they say no, no you need to have one,

00:21:05.180 --> 00:21:07.724
what kind of corporation
doesn't have a president?

00:21:07.724 --> 00:21:09.059
- So, it's about engagement.

00:21:09.059 --> 00:21:11.144
It's about folks having control

00:21:11.144 --> 00:21:12.479
over the workplace

00:21:12.479 --> 00:21:14.022
as much as they also,

00:21:14.022 --> 00:21:15.274
end up in a position of the workplace

00:21:15.274 --> 00:21:18.652
having control over them right?

00:21:18.652 --> 00:21:22.114
- At the end of the day
we're people helping people

00:21:22.114 --> 00:21:23.740
and I think that's how a lot of

00:21:23.740 --> 00:21:26.118
my coworkers view this job

00:21:26.118 --> 00:21:28.287
and if you're coming into this job

00:21:28.287 --> 00:21:29.579
from a non-cooperative structure,

00:21:29.579 --> 00:21:31.039
then it can be very different,

00:21:31.039 --> 00:21:34.167
but I do think it encourages folks to

00:21:34.167 --> 00:21:35.502
view their work differently

00:21:35.502 --> 00:21:38.672
and therefore work in a different way.

00:21:40.132 --> 00:21:41.800
- We're not taught to cooperate,

00:21:41.800 --> 00:21:44.094
and we're not taught to trust.

00:21:44.094 --> 00:21:45.971
Think of all the board games
that we play when we were kids.

00:21:45.971 --> 00:21:48.557
I just remember playing
with my cousins and you got,

00:21:48.557 --> 00:21:49.933
Monopoly,

00:21:49.933 --> 00:21:52.394
it's about being at the top.

00:21:52.394 --> 00:21:53.312
- Working together is--

00:21:53.312 --> 00:21:57.399
- Working together is
really hard. (laughs)

00:21:57.399 --> 00:21:58.442
When everyone has...

00:21:58.442 --> 00:22:01.153
I mean the purpose of a worker co-op is

00:22:01.153 --> 00:22:03.864
democracy between workers.

00:22:03.864 --> 00:22:05.782
So, when everyone has an equal voice,

00:22:05.782 --> 00:22:09.244
it can be difficult to reach a consensus.

00:22:09.244 --> 00:22:11.705
But yeah, basically human
interaction is hard,

00:22:11.705 --> 00:22:14.041
especially when there's
not a hierarchy saying

00:22:14.041 --> 00:22:17.627
you know, I'm the manager,
this is what we're gonna do.

00:22:17.627 --> 00:22:18.587
- [David] We can all work together

00:22:18.587 --> 00:22:20.422
for somebody else's goals.

00:22:20.422 --> 00:22:23.383
So, cooperation to me means

00:22:23.383 --> 00:22:25.927
deciding on those goals together,

00:22:25.927 --> 00:22:28.764
and maybe the benefit of
having a brewery is that

00:22:28.764 --> 00:22:31.641
you get together and you have kind of that

00:22:31.641 --> 00:22:35.562
Habermasian cafe style
where everybody debates.

00:22:36.396 --> 00:22:38.607
That's the democracy,

00:22:38.607 --> 00:22:41.526
and not a voice that's just
understood through a vote,

00:22:41.526 --> 00:22:44.613
but a true voice at the table.

00:22:44.613 --> 00:22:46.740
- Well we're having a baby in

00:22:46.740 --> 00:22:48.700
a few months.
- 4 1/2 months.

00:22:48.700 --> 00:22:50.702
- Yep, really soon, coming up really fast.

00:22:50.702 --> 00:22:52.954
You know this has
brought another aspect of

00:22:52.954 --> 00:22:55.499
long term planning into our co-op meetings

00:22:55.499 --> 00:22:58.251
and the development of our co-op.

00:22:59.878 --> 00:23:01.671
What does maternity leave look like?

00:23:01.671 --> 00:23:03.090
Well, we have to decide that right?

00:23:03.090 --> 00:23:05.926
What does paternity leave look like?

00:23:05.926 --> 00:23:08.178
Is it going to be possible for me

00:23:08.178 --> 00:23:10.972
to step away from my daily job for

00:23:10.972 --> 00:23:13.725
three, four months, maybe longer?

00:23:14.643 --> 00:23:17.104
Which brings us to another complication,

00:23:17.104 --> 00:23:19.398
which is where do we find more people

00:23:19.398 --> 00:23:21.691
with the you know the vision that we have,

00:23:21.691 --> 00:23:24.611
the dedication we have to our work?

00:23:25.487 --> 00:23:26.738
- Who was I speaking to the other day,

00:23:26.738 --> 00:23:29.032
they said never get in
business with your friends

00:23:29.032 --> 00:23:29.991
and it's just like

00:23:29.991 --> 00:23:32.661
I don't know who I'd
wanna work with otherwise

00:23:32.661 --> 00:23:34.788
because if they don't know you,

00:23:34.788 --> 00:23:36.123
if they don't know your story,

00:23:36.123 --> 00:23:39.459
then how do they understand
where you're coming from?

00:23:39.459 --> 00:23:41.253
It's running at 100, though
with just one element.

00:23:41.253 --> 00:23:43.672
- 100 with one is (mumbling).

00:23:43.672 --> 00:23:47.509
But I thought a couple
boilers is all right.

00:23:47.509 --> 00:23:49.636
(baby crying)

00:23:49.636 --> 00:23:51.346
- [David] It can be a
real difficulty to be

00:23:51.346 --> 00:23:53.473
cooperative at all times, right?

00:23:53.473 --> 00:23:55.684
To work in a democratic way

00:23:55.684 --> 00:23:58.186
and to make sure that everyone's on board.

00:23:58.186 --> 00:23:59.855
How long does it take to--

00:23:59.855 --> 00:24:02.899
- It takes 40 minutes in total.

00:24:02.899 --> 00:24:04.734
- [David] You know it's, again

00:24:04.734 --> 00:24:06.903
a real reality in a corporate world.

00:24:06.903 --> 00:24:09.448
You have a child and you
need to take time off?

00:24:09.448 --> 00:24:11.908
You're falling behind.

00:24:11.908 --> 00:24:14.244
- Do you wanna baby piece of toast?

00:24:14.244 --> 00:24:16.830
- It might be easier to do if it were

00:24:16.830 --> 00:24:19.291
just a straight up business sometimes,

00:24:19.291 --> 00:24:20.625
if you didn't have to
deal with relationships

00:24:20.625 --> 00:24:21.835
and you didn't have to
deal with cooperation

00:24:21.835 --> 00:24:23.545
you might be able to do something

00:24:23.545 --> 00:24:25.088
that was far more efficient,

00:24:25.088 --> 00:24:26.965
than what we have but it wouldn't,

00:24:26.965 --> 00:24:31.136
we wouldn't be the ones doing
it, it'd be somebody else.

00:24:40.145 --> 00:24:41.980
- Well, I had kinda come to,

00:24:41.980 --> 00:24:44.357
I guess like the end of my rope

00:24:44.357 --> 00:24:47.194
with job hunting and had
applied for several things

00:24:47.194 --> 00:24:48.695
and wasn't really getting anywhere

00:24:48.695 --> 00:24:50.655
and saw this job posting

00:24:50.655 --> 00:24:52.699
and then I was like oh well somebody who

00:24:52.699 --> 00:24:55.076
knows how to work with
food and can talk to chefs.

00:24:55.076 --> 00:24:57.370
I'm like, well that's
pretty much what I've

00:24:57.370 --> 00:24:58.955
been doing my whole life so,

00:24:58.955 --> 00:25:03.084
that job has got my name
written all over it.

00:25:03.084 --> 00:25:05.378
I guess technically I'm a sales person

00:25:05.378 --> 00:25:07.506
but maybe this is why I'm good at sales

00:25:07.506 --> 00:25:09.341
is 'cause I don't like sales.

00:25:09.341 --> 00:25:13.845
I just let the quality of
what we do speak for itself,

00:25:13.845 --> 00:25:16.181
and they either want it
or they don't want it.

00:25:16.181 --> 00:25:19.643
I don't wanna sell something
to someone who doesn't want it.

00:25:19.643 --> 00:25:20.894
It's dishonest.

00:25:25.649 --> 00:25:28.318
- My job is what I call quality control.

00:25:28.318 --> 00:25:31.112
So, I mean everything you see here

00:25:31.112 --> 00:25:32.322
I make sure that

00:25:32.322 --> 00:25:36.409
I go through everything
before it comes here so,

00:25:36.409 --> 00:25:39.287
that means everything's
as best as possible

00:25:39.287 --> 00:25:41.957
and as perfect as possible.

00:25:41.957 --> 00:25:44.167
So, one of the things that
Sole Food's always been about,

00:25:44.167 --> 00:25:47.087
it's always been about quality.

00:25:47.087 --> 00:25:51.550
We will not put anything out
that we wouldn't take ourselves

00:25:51.550 --> 00:25:55.720
or more likely better than
we would eat ourselves.

00:25:56.972 --> 00:25:59.015
- Another bonus I get that
some people don't get,

00:25:59.015 --> 00:26:01.726
I get to work the market.

00:26:01.726 --> 00:26:02.936
So, I see that finished product

00:26:02.936 --> 00:26:05.438
and then I see the people
that are buying that product

00:26:05.438 --> 00:26:06.898
for a really good price

00:26:06.898 --> 00:26:09.985
and how happy they are with it.

00:26:09.985 --> 00:26:14.155
People are happy with our
product and I love that.

00:26:15.407 --> 00:26:17.117
- One thing that's so great
about Sole Food Farms is

00:26:17.117 --> 00:26:18.618
I think that they know their community.

00:26:18.618 --> 00:26:21.413
They know their neighborhood.

00:26:21.413 --> 00:26:23.665
That's why it's important
that they're nearby,

00:26:23.665 --> 00:26:25.584
that's why, another thing
why we choose them is

00:26:25.584 --> 00:26:26.876
they get it.

00:26:26.876 --> 00:26:29.754
You know they understand the
difficulties that we have.

00:26:29.754 --> 00:26:31.840
We're very, very small first of all

00:26:31.840 --> 00:26:34.175
and every day the menu's different.

00:26:34.175 --> 00:26:37.804
It's what's ever in
season, whatever's fresh,

00:26:37.804 --> 00:26:39.973
whatever they're proud of they send to us

00:26:39.973 --> 00:26:42.976
and then we use that as inspiration.

00:26:54.029 --> 00:26:56.990
- [Michael] It's embodied in how you feel

00:26:56.990 --> 00:26:57.824
when you've done this work.

00:26:57.824 --> 00:26:59.659
How you feel when you see somebody

00:26:59.659 --> 00:27:00.702
who's eaten the food

00:27:00.702 --> 00:27:03.330
and comes back and tells
you how incredible it is.

00:27:03.330 --> 00:27:05.415
Those are the things
that are so intangible

00:27:05.415 --> 00:27:07.667
and so difficult to put a value on.

00:27:07.667 --> 00:27:10.086
You had those before?
- My first time.

00:27:10.086 --> 00:27:14.299
- Oh man let me see what you've got here.

00:27:14.299 --> 00:27:16.885
Oh yeah, that's gonna be good.

00:27:16.885 --> 00:27:19.554
(mellow music)

00:27:21.222 --> 00:27:22.641
These overpasses,

00:27:23.933 --> 00:27:26.144
they're gonna come down in four years

00:27:26.144 --> 00:27:29.105
and when they do, where we sit right now,

00:27:29.105 --> 00:27:32.400
where there's these
wonderful crops growing,

00:27:32.400 --> 00:27:35.153
the carrots and the
radicchio and the arugula and

00:27:35.153 --> 00:27:36.696
parsleys and whatever's here

00:27:36.696 --> 00:27:38.948
will be replaced with high rises

