00:30:48.012 --> 00:30:50.265
So, we gotta clean that up.

00:30:57.105 --> 00:30:58.731
- You need small businesses

00:30:58.731 --> 00:31:01.150
that may not have a ton of
capital in order to buy it

00:31:01.150 --> 00:31:03.778
to at least have a
responsible expectation that

00:31:03.778 --> 00:31:05.738
the building won't be
swept out from under them

00:31:05.738 --> 00:31:09.909
as soon as it becomes
profitable to somebody else.

00:31:14.622 --> 00:31:16.916
- This neighborhood
provides a real opportunity

00:31:16.916 --> 00:31:20.670
in that it can become,
if we want it to be,

00:31:20.670 --> 00:31:23.381
it can become a place
where all are accepted

00:31:23.381 --> 00:31:25.633
where we have a mixed neighborhood

00:31:25.633 --> 00:31:28.094
where people from all income brackets,

00:31:28.094 --> 00:31:31.514
all walks, all backgrounds
have a capacity to interact,

00:31:31.514 --> 00:31:33.308
and build a good life.

00:31:33.308 --> 00:31:35.643
That's what we're aiming for.

00:31:35.643 --> 00:31:38.396
There are some pretty big

00:31:38.396 --> 00:31:40.398
obstacles in our way and

00:31:42.400 --> 00:31:46.571
quite directly it's those
who control the property

00:31:47.488 --> 00:31:48.740
are in our way.

00:31:51.159 --> 00:31:53.620
- [David] It's not money, I mean money is,

00:31:53.620 --> 00:31:55.288
it's a necessity in this world,

00:31:55.288 --> 00:31:56.873
but that's truly not the relationships

00:31:56.873 --> 00:31:58.708
we're trying to build.

00:32:00.877 --> 00:32:03.212
- Collaboration is ultimately

00:32:03.212 --> 00:32:05.715
the more sustainable approach

00:32:05.715 --> 00:32:07.550
than competition,

00:32:07.550 --> 00:32:09.761
whereas of course our
competitive capitalist system,

00:32:09.761 --> 00:32:11.346
in Wall Street no more than anywhere,

00:32:11.346 --> 00:32:13.848
is rooted in the idea that you know this,

00:32:13.848 --> 00:32:15.767
this false understanding of Darwin.

00:32:15.767 --> 00:32:18.186
What Darwin said is the fittest...

00:32:18.186 --> 00:32:19.687
In fact he didn't even coin the term

00:32:19.687 --> 00:32:21.022
survival of the fittest,

00:32:21.022 --> 00:32:22.482
but the way he used it is

00:32:22.482 --> 00:32:24.734
fittest as in the one that fits best

00:32:24.734 --> 00:32:28.363
within the whole system,
not the, the strongest.

00:32:28.363 --> 00:32:31.032
(mellow music)

00:32:36.913 --> 00:32:39.123
- So, my name is Tiberius Brastaviceanu.

00:32:39.123 --> 00:32:41.209
People know me as Tibi.

00:32:41.209 --> 00:32:45.630
When I first came to
Canada I was 14 years old

00:32:45.630 --> 00:32:47.548
and I went to University of Montreal

00:32:47.548 --> 00:32:49.717
for a masters in Applied Physics.

00:32:49.717 --> 00:32:54.430
Laser applications to
biology and (mumbles).

00:32:54.430 --> 00:32:56.766
I work here at the Sensorica Lab.

00:32:56.766 --> 00:33:00.019
Sensorica is this new
type of organization where

00:33:00.019 --> 00:33:01.270
you're free to join,

00:33:01.270 --> 00:33:03.856
and to take initiative
and start new projects.

00:33:03.856 --> 00:33:06.025
It is a commercial venture,

00:33:06.025 --> 00:33:09.112
and you work collaboratively with people.

00:33:09.112 --> 00:33:13.491
You share the revenue according
to everyone's contribution.

00:33:13.491 --> 00:33:18.413
It is a decentralized
network and there is no boss.

00:33:18.413 --> 00:33:21.374
We focus on material production,

00:33:21.374 --> 00:33:23.334
meaning hardware,

00:33:23.334 --> 00:33:26.129
electronics, we put stuff together.

00:33:26.129 --> 00:33:27.839
So, we're trying to solve how

00:33:27.839 --> 00:33:30.758
material stuff could be
produced in an open way.

00:33:30.758 --> 00:33:34.721
Sensorica started by focusing
on open source hardware

00:33:34.721 --> 00:33:36.472
but on the high end side.

00:33:36.472 --> 00:33:39.058
So, we said let's first
prove that open source

00:33:39.058 --> 00:33:41.853
can produce scientific instruments.

00:33:41.853 --> 00:33:43.938
Five years ago open source
hardware was just starting,

00:33:43.938 --> 00:33:46.774
now we see drones and 3D printers.

00:33:47.859 --> 00:33:50.695
These two industries are
driven by open source.

00:33:50.695 --> 00:33:53.614
(machine whirring)

00:33:55.450 --> 00:33:58.619
This is the main hub of the
network where things happen,

00:33:58.619 --> 00:34:02.623
where hardware gets done,
prototyped, and produced.

00:34:02.623 --> 00:34:05.168
There's other people working
from their own little

00:34:05.168 --> 00:34:06.919
home labs around the world

00:34:06.919 --> 00:34:09.505
that do some prototyping
and then we mirror it here.

00:34:09.505 --> 00:34:12.258
We're democratizing here
access to the micron world.

00:34:12.258 --> 00:34:15.428
Traditionally these pieces can cost you

00:34:15.428 --> 00:34:16.846
tens of thousands of dollars.

00:34:16.846 --> 00:34:17.847
Open source,

00:34:19.432 --> 00:34:22.018
maybe 20% of the cost,

00:34:22.018 --> 00:34:26.689
allow scientific labs in
universities in the emerging world

00:34:26.689 --> 00:34:29.150
to do their science.

00:34:29.150 --> 00:34:30.735
My background is in lasers, so

00:34:30.735 --> 00:34:33.237
everything you see with
optics, optical fiber,

00:34:33.237 --> 00:34:35.323
that's my contribution to the projects,

00:34:35.323 --> 00:34:37.408
and the mechanical side
and the electronics part

00:34:37.408 --> 00:34:40.286
these are other people that
have contributed to it.

00:34:40.286 --> 00:34:42.789
It's a collaborative work, I think.

00:34:42.789 --> 00:34:46.209
There's many different sections here.

00:34:46.209 --> 00:34:48.669
So, this is our micro 3D printer

00:34:48.669 --> 00:34:50.505
which will be used for

00:34:51.631 --> 00:34:53.883
micro 3D chips design.

00:34:53.883 --> 00:34:56.177
What we do, we add an optical fiber.

00:34:56.177 --> 00:34:57.887
It's just the size of a little hair.

00:34:57.887 --> 00:34:59.847
So, this is like a pen of light

00:34:59.847 --> 00:35:02.350
that solidifies or polymerizes

00:35:04.143 --> 00:35:06.145
the resin that you put inside,

00:35:06.145 --> 00:35:08.606
and you're able to do tiny,
tiny, tiny structures.

00:35:08.606 --> 00:35:09.816
It just needs a bit of integration,

00:35:09.816 --> 00:35:11.317
but the proof of concept is done

00:35:11.317 --> 00:35:12.693
so, that's something that will go on

00:35:12.693 --> 00:35:14.112
crowd funding pretty soon.

00:35:14.112 --> 00:35:18.116
The tiniest printer in the world. (laughs)

00:35:18.116 --> 00:35:19.951
People here are motivated by

00:35:19.951 --> 00:35:22.912
what's the next big thing we can work on

00:35:22.912 --> 00:35:24.997
that can be so disruptive

00:35:26.124 --> 00:35:28.584
from micro 3D devices
and democratizing testing

00:35:28.584 --> 00:35:30.962
of food and water quality,

00:35:30.962 --> 00:35:33.589
how to automate a garden,
how to automate a greenhouse.

00:35:33.589 --> 00:35:35.591
Well, here's all the
electronics that you need

00:35:35.591 --> 00:35:37.135
and the software to run it.

00:35:37.135 --> 00:35:40.138
We affectionately call
this the iPot. (laughs)

00:35:40.138 --> 00:35:43.057
A completely autonomous flower pot.

00:35:43.891 --> 00:35:47.270
We have an atmospheric
temperature and humidity sensor

00:35:47.270 --> 00:35:50.523
so, the plant knows if the air is dry

00:35:50.523 --> 00:35:53.317
or if the air is very humid,
and it knows the temperature.

00:35:53.317 --> 00:35:55.778
And here we have a light sensor

00:35:55.778 --> 00:35:59.073
so, the plant knows when
to turn the LED off/on

00:35:59.073 --> 00:36:01.284
based on how much light
it got during the day.

00:36:01.284 --> 00:36:02.493
You know the scientific instruments

00:36:02.493 --> 00:36:04.954
that reduce the cost of innovation allows

00:36:04.954 --> 00:36:07.707
people in Africa, and
Asia, and South America

00:36:07.707 --> 00:36:10.835
to catch up with scientific development,

00:36:10.835 --> 00:36:12.670
so, I think we're highly motivated

00:36:12.670 --> 00:36:14.422
by these kind of projects.

00:36:14.422 --> 00:36:16.132
The real change happens when

00:36:16.132 --> 00:36:18.176
economic relationships changed,

00:36:18.176 --> 00:36:22.638
and so, then the idea was
let's set up new economic

00:36:22.638 --> 00:36:26.851
organizations based on
these new principles

00:36:26.851 --> 00:36:30.396
by respecting some fundamental principles

00:36:30.396 --> 00:36:32.773
that were behind what
was already existing,

00:36:32.773 --> 00:36:35.026
so, let's build the
enterprise of the future.

00:36:35.026 --> 00:36:39.197
And that was the seed value
for the Sensorica network.

00:36:41.991 --> 00:36:43.242
- First you have to

00:36:43.242 --> 00:36:46.412
release all the air you have

00:36:46.412 --> 00:36:48.623
so, you can jump into the water

00:36:48.623 --> 00:36:53.169
and then you can try to
catch the stars by exhaling.

00:36:53.169 --> 00:36:56.923
Breathing Games is a project
we started for children

00:36:56.923 --> 00:36:58.674
with cystic fibrosis.

00:36:58.674 --> 00:37:01.636
Blow, blow, blow, blow, or
you're gonna hit the banan.

00:37:01.636 --> 00:37:02.678
Ah, you hit him.

00:37:02.678 --> 00:37:06.098
It's really about making the therapy fun.

00:37:06.098 --> 00:37:08.726
Having the therapy is quite the challenge

00:37:08.726 --> 00:37:11.229
for chronic illnesses,

00:37:11.229 --> 00:37:15.441
and about one person over two
doesn't do the therapy well.

00:37:15.441 --> 00:37:17.485
It's an exercise they have to do daily,

00:37:17.485 --> 00:37:20.655
so it's quite tedious and yeah boring.

00:37:22.156 --> 00:37:24.700
So, the games make the,

00:37:24.700 --> 00:37:27.495
the therapy fun but it also
gives the feedback to them

00:37:27.495 --> 00:37:29.538
about you're breathing

00:37:29.538 --> 00:37:31.207
too low or too high.

00:37:32.833 --> 00:37:37.421
We just connect this medical
device to the computer

00:37:37.421 --> 00:37:39.966
so, that we can use the data

00:37:39.966 --> 00:37:43.135
for the game and for the research too.

00:37:44.136 --> 00:37:47.139
For hospitals, it's costing at least

00:37:48.307 --> 00:37:49.141
$1,500.

00:37:50.476 --> 00:37:53.521
So, it's not something you
can have at home easily.

00:37:53.521 --> 00:37:56.524
If we include all the
parts that we plan to add

00:37:56.524 --> 00:38:00.069
such as the memory card
and the battery and so,

00:38:00.069 --> 00:38:01.988
it would be about $200.

00:38:03.364 --> 00:38:06.826
In a later phase, we
want to do a microchip

00:38:06.826 --> 00:38:11.414
and it would reduce the
cost to maybe $60, $80.

00:38:11.414 --> 00:38:15.584
- The cost of innovation
using open source is very low.

00:38:15.584 --> 00:38:20.339
These products can be
10% of the market price.

00:38:20.339 --> 00:38:23.968
So, you're sinking the
costs of innovation,

00:38:23.968 --> 00:38:28.097
and you're taking away some
costs of production or assembly.

00:38:28.097 --> 00:38:31.600
Here's the file, print it at your local

00:38:31.600 --> 00:38:36.022
3D printing shop and then
put it together IKEA style.

00:38:36.022 --> 00:38:39.025
It really reduces the costs of a product.

00:38:39.025 --> 00:38:41.110
- With like open licenses,

00:38:41.110 --> 00:38:44.447
someone could take this and improve it,

00:38:44.447 --> 00:38:46.198
and re-share it.

00:38:46.198 --> 00:38:47.908
- Once it's completed,

00:38:47.908 --> 00:38:50.161
the how to is gonna be
put out to the world,

00:38:50.161 --> 00:38:52.455
and anyone that has the
capacity to replicate

00:38:52.455 --> 00:38:54.206
the device can do it?

00:38:54.206 --> 00:38:55.833
- Yes.
- No cost?

00:38:55.833 --> 00:38:58.169
But there's also, going to be potentially

00:38:58.169 --> 00:38:59.545
a commercial aspect to it.

00:38:59.545 --> 00:39:00.671
How's that gonna work?

00:39:00.671 --> 00:39:04.633
- The question is open source hardware

00:39:04.633 --> 00:39:05.468
a viable

00:39:06.886 --> 00:39:08.763
you know business model?

00:39:08.763 --> 00:39:11.182
You have something at home to
bake something, to bake bread?

00:39:11.182 --> 00:39:12.266
- [Man] Oh yes I do.

00:39:12.266 --> 00:39:13.392
- Everybody has right?

00:39:13.392 --> 00:39:14.769
Why don't you make your own bread?

00:39:14.769 --> 00:39:17.229
You find tons of recipes on the internet,

00:39:17.229 --> 00:39:18.564
why don't you make it?

00:39:18.564 --> 00:39:21.901
You know and then you
make money on convenience,

00:39:21.901 --> 00:39:25.029
you say "I can source the
parts for you, put 'em in a bag

00:39:25.029 --> 00:39:26.280
"and send them to you."

00:39:26.280 --> 00:39:31.160
Open source how their businesses
also survive on services.

00:39:31.160 --> 00:39:33.496
People are asking, "Yeah
but if you can't patent,

00:39:33.496 --> 00:39:34.372
"how can you protect?

00:39:34.372 --> 00:39:35.664
"People are gonna copy you."

00:39:35.664 --> 00:39:38.292
- You might gather your
thing to protect your idea

00:39:38.292 --> 00:39:39.502
and develop it,

00:39:39.502 --> 00:39:42.922
okay but then you patent
it and you protect it,

00:39:42.922 --> 00:39:45.341
what if tomorrow somebody

00:39:45.341 --> 00:39:47.927
thinks of something that
you guys didn't think of

00:39:47.927 --> 00:39:50.429
and improves it and all of the sudden

00:39:50.429 --> 00:39:52.848
releases their new technology

00:39:53.849 --> 00:39:56.310
that is better than yours
so, yours is obsolete.

00:39:56.310 --> 00:39:57.561
So, your patent

00:39:59.313 --> 00:40:00.231
is useless.

00:40:02.400 --> 00:40:03.651
- This is a journey.

00:40:03.651 --> 00:40:05.403
We're on a journey to figure this out.

00:40:05.403 --> 00:40:06.987
I'm pretty confident that

00:40:06.987 --> 00:40:09.824
the regenerative frame is the right frame

00:40:09.824 --> 00:40:13.077
and a regenerative frame
means that a system

00:40:13.077 --> 00:40:15.996
is self-organizing, self-refueling,

00:40:17.123 --> 00:40:18.624
self-learning

00:40:18.624 --> 00:40:21.168
and so, you know, we see it

00:40:21.168 --> 00:40:23.504
in lots of projects in the world.

00:40:23.504 --> 00:40:24.755
It is emergent.

00:40:26.132 --> 00:40:27.591
- [Patricia] Did we mention not to

00:40:27.591 --> 00:40:30.261
vibrate here or are we vibrating?

00:40:30.261 --> 00:40:32.430
- No vibrato? No vibrato okay.

00:40:32.430 --> 00:40:33.931
Yeah okay let's, maybe let's try that.

00:40:33.931 --> 00:40:35.099
- You wanna try that?
- Yeah let's try that.

00:40:35.099 --> 00:40:37.351
Let's try it from the top.

00:40:37.351 --> 00:40:40.020
(chamber music)

00:40:42.481 --> 00:40:46.569
- A good listener is somebody
who can understand meaning

00:40:46.569 --> 00:40:49.238
beyond what people actually say.

00:40:55.661 --> 00:40:59.874
Very few of us actually
can find right away

00:40:59.874 --> 00:41:01.750
particularly the words that

00:41:01.750 --> 00:41:03.544
we want to use

00:41:03.544 --> 00:41:05.546
to explain what we mean.

00:41:06.714 --> 00:41:09.341
And a good listening skill

00:41:09.341 --> 00:41:10.384
is

00:41:10.384 --> 00:41:12.052
the ability to sense

00:41:13.137 --> 00:41:14.221
what's behind

00:41:15.264 --> 00:41:17.725
what people obviously

00:41:17.725 --> 00:41:18.976
say.

00:41:18.976 --> 00:41:20.144
- If you're playing a faster tempo,

00:41:20.144 --> 00:41:21.729
then we can't fit
together, it's not working.

00:41:21.729 --> 00:41:22.855
- [Patricia] Yeah, and I
think Yuel's in between us,

00:41:22.855 --> 00:41:24.398
so it's real hard for him too.

00:41:24.398 --> 00:41:27.109
- Yeah well let's try
it again, see if we can

00:41:27.109 --> 00:41:29.737
feel this a little bit
more together as a group.

00:41:29.737 --> 00:41:32.406
(chamber music)

00:41:45.878 --> 00:41:48.172
- If you were describing
Loomio to your brother,

00:41:48.172 --> 00:41:51.842
I think you would say something like

00:41:51.842 --> 00:41:56.722
Loomio is a simple technology
that enables people to

00:41:56.722 --> 00:42:00.309
come together to talk an issue through,

00:42:00.309 --> 00:42:01.852
generate ideas,

00:42:01.852 --> 00:42:04.897
and come to a clear, shared
understanding together,

00:42:04.897 --> 00:42:09.443
and a decision about what
action to take together.

00:42:09.443 --> 00:42:10.819
The idea came out of our experience

00:42:10.819 --> 00:42:12.571
during the Occupy movement

00:42:12.571 --> 00:42:16.242
of the potential for
collaborative decision making.

00:42:16.242 --> 00:42:17.910
When the process is well held,

00:42:17.910 --> 00:42:19.495
it can be totally amazing

00:42:19.495 --> 00:42:21.205
like transformative for people.

00:42:21.205 --> 00:42:24.124
When diverse perspectives
continually fade into a

00:42:24.124 --> 00:42:25.417
collaborative process,

00:42:25.417 --> 00:42:27.920
you know groups can end up
making more intelligent decisions

00:42:27.920 --> 00:42:31.215
than any individual would by themselves.

00:42:32.675 --> 00:42:34.301
- At the Red Victorian we

00:42:34.301 --> 00:42:38.222
invite many people to be part
of the decision making process

00:42:38.222 --> 00:42:41.225
and the way that we do that
is we provide them a link to

00:42:41.225 --> 00:42:42.601
Loomio.

00:42:42.601 --> 00:42:45.020
I'd love to get people to chime
in in the next day or two.

00:42:45.020 --> 00:42:47.064
Well yeah, in the next 24 hours

00:42:47.064 --> 00:42:50.067
on the Loomio thread and
then at least I'll have some

00:42:50.067 --> 00:42:52.111
kind of direction to start
a conversation with them.

00:42:52.111 --> 00:42:54.363
Some things are decided fully on Loomio,

00:42:54.363 --> 00:42:57.241
some things involved the
offline component as well.

00:42:57.241 --> 00:42:59.285
For larger issues, we tend to

00:42:59.285 --> 00:43:01.203
then take that Loomio conversation,

00:43:01.203 --> 00:43:02.871
meet in person,

00:43:02.871 --> 00:43:05.958
and kind of go over some of the nuances

00:43:05.958 --> 00:43:07.167
as a group.

00:43:07.167 --> 00:43:09.128
- Oh so, it's like do we
wanna take that battle on

00:43:09.128 --> 00:43:10.379
as our own?

00:43:10.379 --> 00:43:13.465
- So, when a discussion
gets to the point where

00:43:13.465 --> 00:43:14.675
someone in the group thinks

00:43:14.675 --> 00:43:16.260
hey, we can make a decision here.

00:43:16.260 --> 00:43:18.762
I've got an idea, this is what
I think the group should do,

00:43:18.762 --> 00:43:20.889
they can put up a proposal.

00:43:20.889 --> 00:43:23.892
And so, you've got discussion
on the left of the screen

00:43:23.892 --> 00:43:25.728
and decision making on the right

00:43:25.728 --> 00:43:28.397
and a proposal always
has a time limit on it.

00:43:28.397 --> 00:43:31.066
So, it always comes to a clear conclusion.

00:43:31.066 --> 00:43:33.485
So, you've got this sort of divergence

00:43:33.485 --> 00:43:35.446
in the discussion and then you converge

00:43:35.446 --> 00:43:38.240
on a shared outcome, an agreed outcome.

00:43:38.240 --> 00:43:40.451
People participate in a decision

00:43:40.451 --> 00:43:43.037
by clicking one of four buttons.

00:43:43.037 --> 00:43:44.788
So, you can either agree,

00:43:44.788 --> 00:43:47.499
or you can sort of opt out or abstain,

00:43:47.499 --> 00:43:49.543
or you can disagree,

00:43:49.543 --> 00:43:53.005
which is like saying I don't
know that it's the best idea

00:43:53.005 --> 00:43:55.299
but I'm not gonna stand
in the way of the group,

00:43:55.299 --> 00:43:58.469
or you can block or raise a red flag,

00:43:58.469 --> 00:44:02.389
which is saying like I think
this is a really bad idea,

00:44:02.389 --> 00:44:03.932
I've got a serious concern

00:44:03.932 --> 00:44:06.226
that I would like to be addressed

00:44:06.226 --> 00:44:08.979
before we move forward with this.

00:44:10.147 --> 00:44:11.774
- [Jessy Kate] I think that
Loomio is really interesting

00:44:11.774 --> 00:44:14.151
for the fact that it creates space for

00:44:14.151 --> 00:44:17.363
people to kind of take the time
to deal with their emotions,

00:44:17.363 --> 00:44:20.407
and then process them often
in more constructive ways

00:44:20.407 --> 00:44:23.369
by writing down what they're thinking.

00:44:23.369 --> 00:44:26.705
- There's all kinds of awful
stuff that happens online,

00:44:26.705 --> 00:44:27.831
bullying,

00:44:27.831 --> 00:44:31.126
but Loomio's sort of designed with that,

00:44:32.127 --> 00:44:34.046
I guess with this idea in mind that

00:44:34.046 --> 00:44:37.966
if we design online
environments in a way that makes

00:44:37.966 --> 00:44:41.095
certain types of behavior easier,

00:44:41.095 --> 00:44:44.098
so, really fosters a sense of

00:44:44.098 --> 00:44:46.892
respectful, constructive dialogue,

00:44:46.892 --> 00:44:49.812
I mean it's really about people
coming together to achieve

00:44:49.812 --> 00:44:53.023
a shared purpose in a way that works

00:44:53.023 --> 00:44:56.610
for all of the people involved
and it's kind of that simple.

00:44:56.610 --> 00:44:58.821
Little kids get this stuff.

00:44:58.821 --> 00:45:01.907
You know, we have 12-year-old
kids using Loomio effectively

00:45:01.907 --> 00:45:04.243
in their schools with parents and teachers

00:45:04.243 --> 00:45:06.078
making decisions together.

00:45:06.078 --> 00:45:07.621
- I think that Loomio really catalyzes

00:45:07.621 --> 00:45:09.540
those kinds of conversations because

00:45:09.540 --> 00:45:11.834
you pick up on those nuances,
and you start to see them.

00:45:11.834 --> 00:45:13.669
And it's not just actually the technology,

00:45:13.669 --> 00:45:15.254
it's the types of collaborative structures

00:45:15.254 --> 00:45:16.880
that technology enables.

00:45:16.880 --> 00:45:19.925
- This idea that we expect
democracy from our government

00:45:19.925 --> 00:45:21.885
and we're totally fine with like

00:45:21.885 --> 00:45:24.972
tyranny or dictatorship in our
workplaces for some reason,

00:45:24.972 --> 00:45:27.641
even though that's where
we spend eight hours a day

00:45:27.641 --> 00:45:29.476
for most of our lives.

00:45:30.644 --> 00:45:32.229
I have a lot of hope that that's changing.

00:45:32.229 --> 00:45:33.480
It feels like there's a real movement

00:45:33.480 --> 00:45:35.816
building around changing it.

00:45:36.775 --> 00:45:39.194
It's just people coming
together to achieve something

00:45:39.194 --> 00:45:41.739
around the creation of value,

00:45:41.739 --> 00:45:42.948
and the cooperative structure is,

00:45:42.948 --> 00:45:45.659
we've found it to be
totally flexible enough

00:45:45.659 --> 00:45:47.870
to have you know real room to move,

00:45:47.870 --> 00:45:48.954
to be adaptive,

00:45:48.954 --> 00:45:51.206
to set things up in a resilient way

00:45:51.206 --> 00:45:52.916
that doesn't involve specialization,

00:45:52.916 --> 00:45:57.087
and you're most effectively
served the social mission.

00:45:59.506 --> 00:46:00.883
- [Tibi] Sensorica, we found a

00:46:00.883 --> 00:46:03.177
system, which is the
value accounting system,

00:46:03.177 --> 00:46:05.012
where we can log our time.

00:46:05.012 --> 00:46:07.222
And as soon as we have finances,

00:46:07.222 --> 00:46:11.393
we can use the log we created
to redistribute the money.

00:46:11.393 --> 00:46:12.978
- Pizza number one.

00:46:14.104 --> 00:46:15.773
Ladies and gentlemen, pizza number one.

00:46:15.773 --> 00:46:17.065
- Sensorica is a network where

00:46:17.065 --> 00:46:20.027
people can collaborate
on different projects

00:46:20.027 --> 00:46:24.698
and the idea is that the system
that we have put in place

00:46:24.698 --> 00:46:27.618
captures all these contributions
from different individuals

00:46:27.618 --> 00:46:29.828
in the context of that particular project.

00:46:29.828 --> 00:46:33.540
And then we decide how
to share the benefits.

00:46:33.540 --> 00:46:35.959
- According to the value equation.

00:46:35.959 --> 00:46:37.127
- According to a value equation

00:46:37.127 --> 00:46:39.963
which is a social contract that we

00:46:41.715 --> 00:46:44.635
make and we agree on
before the project starts.

00:46:44.635 --> 00:46:46.178
So, there's 5% that goes actually

00:46:46.178 --> 00:46:48.180
to sustain the infrastructure,

00:46:48.180 --> 00:46:50.098
to sustain the labs okay?

00:46:50.098 --> 00:46:53.227
So, let's take this little part here

00:46:53.227 --> 00:46:55.312
and nobody eats this one right?

00:46:55.312 --> 00:46:59.233
And now here's a guy that
contributed let's say

00:47:00.234 --> 00:47:01.068
a quarter.

00:47:02.319 --> 00:47:04.822
Our system allows anybody
in the world to contribute

00:47:04.822 --> 00:47:07.324
as much or as little as they want.

00:47:07.324 --> 00:47:09.701
So, somebody's gonna
get just a little piece

00:47:09.701 --> 00:47:10.911
and that's fine.

00:47:10.911 --> 00:47:12.496
Because this guy is probably
doing something else,

00:47:12.496 --> 00:47:13.664
he's working on other projects,

00:47:13.664 --> 00:47:16.083
and that's his little contribution.

00:47:16.083 --> 00:47:17.334
Here I'm gonna take this piece.

00:47:17.334 --> 00:47:20.462
I'm gonna be modest.
- So conservative.

00:47:24.216 --> 00:47:27.052
- How I got to the participatory economy

00:47:27.052 --> 00:47:31.682
is by having been shocked
with my previous job.

00:47:31.682 --> 00:47:35.143
In 2006, I left Montreal
to the Silicon Valley

00:47:35.143 --> 00:47:36.186
in San Francisco,

00:47:36.186 --> 00:47:38.105
and this is where I got my first job

00:47:38.105 --> 00:47:41.400
as an engineer in a company
manufacturing lasers.

00:47:41.400 --> 00:47:42.860
I was very stimulated.

00:47:42.860 --> 00:47:46.363
I put a lot of heart into the work,

00:47:46.363 --> 00:47:49.283
and then 2008 financial crisis comes,

00:47:49.283 --> 00:47:52.369
and then you see how people are treated

00:47:52.369 --> 00:47:54.413
once they're not

00:47:54.413 --> 00:47:55.831
an asset anymore.

00:47:56.832 --> 00:47:59.418
So, I just got laid off
and kicked out essentially.

00:47:59.418 --> 00:48:01.962
That led me to use the
internet to find solutions,

00:48:01.962 --> 00:48:03.338
and I realized how much power

00:48:03.338 --> 00:48:06.717
the internet can give to an
individual and to groups.

00:48:06.717 --> 00:48:10.178
So, that frustration turned
into something constructive.

00:48:10.178 --> 00:48:11.555
I got the pictures.

00:48:11.555 --> 00:48:13.015
- [Man] Yeah I'm trying
to see if there's more.

00:48:13.015 --> 00:48:14.933
Well you're, you're there are you there?

00:48:14.933 --> 00:48:16.268
- [Tibi] When I first came to Canada,

00:48:16.268 --> 00:48:18.312
I just knew a bit of English,

00:48:18.312 --> 00:48:19.146
not much,

00:48:20.230 --> 00:48:22.900
and I didn't speak French.

00:48:22.900 --> 00:48:25.068
Canada was pretty open to

00:48:25.068 --> 00:48:27.613
immigration from Romania back then.

00:48:27.613 --> 00:48:29.990
I think the second
community that adopted us

00:48:29.990 --> 00:48:31.700
was the Italian community.

00:48:31.700 --> 00:48:32.618
We spoke Italian,

00:48:32.618 --> 00:48:34.244
and they were more
established than Romanians

00:48:34.244 --> 00:48:35.454
who were just arriving.

00:48:35.454 --> 00:48:37.915
- [Man] So you started
here, what, bus boy first?

00:48:37.915 --> 00:48:39.207
- Yeah, for sure, for sure.

00:48:39.207 --> 00:48:40.584
I didn't speak French or English.

00:48:40.584 --> 00:48:42.252
I just started working here.

00:48:42.252 --> 00:48:45.464
And then slowly, slowly I
started to pick up orders.

00:48:45.464 --> 00:48:47.925
Tarly was here every week.

00:48:47.925 --> 00:48:49.426
- Those were the days my friend.

00:48:49.426 --> 00:48:50.719
Beautiful days.

00:48:50.719 --> 00:48:52.304
Now all of a sudden you got smart.

00:48:52.304 --> 00:48:55.933
Whatever you do, I hope it makes a change.

00:48:55.933 --> 00:48:58.393
- [Tibi] What do you think about change?

00:48:58.393 --> 00:49:02.731
- If it comes from you
it's probably a good thing.

00:49:02.731 --> 00:49:04.691
- What a beautiful day huh?

00:49:04.691 --> 00:49:07.277
(speaking foreign language)

00:49:07.277 --> 00:49:08.987
My father's place from in Romania,

00:49:08.987 --> 00:49:10.489
it's the capital of watermelon.

00:49:10.489 --> 00:49:12.658
So, this is something we eat every day.

00:49:12.658 --> 00:49:13.909
- [Interviewer] Do you need help?

00:49:13.909 --> 00:49:15.077
Do you need help eating it?

00:49:15.077 --> 00:49:16.078
- Yes, yes go ahead.

00:49:16.078 --> 00:49:18.205
It drives my mother
crazy the way we eat it.

00:49:18.205 --> 00:49:20.832
(everyone chatting)

00:49:20.832 --> 00:49:22.459
I knew the truth since I was young,

00:49:22.459 --> 00:49:24.336
because my father was very open about it.

00:49:24.336 --> 00:49:28.548
My father was imprisoned in
Romania when he was young.

00:49:28.548 --> 00:49:30.509
He always stood up against the regime,

00:49:30.509 --> 00:49:32.928
so I grew up in an
environment where, you know,

00:49:32.928 --> 00:49:34.680
idealism comes first.

00:49:36.807 --> 00:49:39.226
They're very, very supportive parents.

00:49:39.226 --> 00:49:41.812
You know I'll be speaking
about what people have done

00:49:41.812 --> 00:49:44.523
for society so it runs deep.

00:49:44.523 --> 00:49:49.444
How do you say father in--
(speaking foreign language)

00:49:49.444 --> 00:49:52.155
They just dropped everything
to flee the country,

00:49:52.155 --> 00:49:53.907
and that was to give us a better future.

00:49:53.907 --> 00:49:56.576
(chamber music)

00:50:09.506 --> 00:50:11.967
- People actually come to this

00:50:11.967 --> 00:50:13.802
kind of notion of,

00:50:13.802 --> 00:50:16.013
and I call it building the next society,

00:50:16.013 --> 00:50:18.598
building the next system.

00:50:18.598 --> 00:50:21.143
They come to it from different reasons.

00:50:21.143 --> 00:50:24.021
Some come out of a lot
of pain and poverty,

00:50:24.021 --> 00:50:26.481
and excruciating difficulties.

00:50:26.481 --> 00:50:29.693
Either try something new
or we're in real trouble

00:50:29.693 --> 00:50:32.362
The other part of it I think is,

00:50:33.905 --> 00:50:36.033
it's cultural but it's also, people

00:50:36.033 --> 00:50:39.244
who really crave getting
away from individualism

00:50:39.244 --> 00:50:43.415
somehow, but the hunger for
what community can bring.

00:50:44.791 --> 00:50:47.461
(chamber music)

00:50:52.883 --> 00:50:54.801
- Sometimes we can just
play through the music,

00:50:54.801 --> 00:50:55.969
the first time's not so good,

00:50:55.969 --> 00:50:57.804
but then even without words,

00:50:57.804 --> 00:51:00.098
it evolves into something.

00:51:00.098 --> 00:51:03.393
I mean we're just all giving
and taking at the same time.

00:51:03.393 --> 00:51:06.063
(mellow music)

00:51:16.490 --> 00:51:18.200
- The mother is in Thorncliffe Park.

00:51:18.200 --> 00:51:23.163
We wanted to make this place
a better place for everyone.

00:51:23.163 --> 00:51:24.915
I'm originally from India,

00:51:24.915 --> 00:51:26.750
and one of my husband's friend,

00:51:26.750 --> 00:51:30.295
he gave a tour of Thorncliffe Park,

00:51:30.295 --> 00:51:34.216
and the first day itself
we felt home here.

00:51:34.216 --> 00:51:36.176
The next day when we got up,

00:51:36.176 --> 00:51:38.178
and we wanted to walk in the neighborhood,

00:51:38.178 --> 00:51:39.638
we walked into the park,

00:51:39.638 --> 00:51:43.225
and then we were really not
happy with the condition

00:51:43.225 --> 00:51:44.976
of the park and all.

00:51:44.976 --> 00:51:48.647
But when we are here,
we have to fix things.

00:51:48.647 --> 00:51:51.900
We came to know about a lot of women who

00:51:51.900 --> 00:51:54.236
live in this apartment buildings,

00:51:54.236 --> 00:51:55.779
mostly the immigrant,

00:51:55.779 --> 00:51:59.116
and they are doing businesses from home.

00:52:00.408 --> 00:52:02.327
They bring stuff from
their home countries,

00:52:02.327 --> 00:52:05.247
and then sell it to
their neighbor's friends

00:52:05.247 --> 00:52:06.748
at a very good price,

00:52:06.748 --> 00:52:08.834
and make a supplement
income for themselves.

00:52:08.834 --> 00:52:11.545
(speaking foreign language)

00:52:11.545 --> 00:52:13.922
- This is for your great grandchildren.

00:52:13.922 --> 00:52:16.842
- When we come together we talk about,

00:52:16.842 --> 00:52:19.678
oh this is our like backyard

00:52:19.678 --> 00:52:24.224
because all years in back
home when we are living,

00:52:24.224 --> 00:52:26.685
house always have backyard or front yard.

00:52:26.685 --> 00:52:27.894
There in the building,

00:52:27.894 --> 00:52:29.855
you don't have any backyard any private.

00:52:29.855 --> 00:52:32.274
So we call this there is a backyard.

00:52:32.274 --> 00:52:36.444
So, in backyard we have
to have some activity too.

00:52:38.155 --> 00:52:42.784
- So, we thought that
this is a really good idea

00:52:42.784 --> 00:52:44.953
to create a market.

00:52:44.953 --> 00:52:47.247
This market will serve as a platform

00:52:47.247 --> 00:52:50.792
for the vendors and these newcomer women

00:52:50.792 --> 00:52:53.253
where they will feel confidence

00:52:53.253 --> 00:52:55.505
and you know and it will also,

00:52:55.505 --> 00:52:58.508
empower them build their self-esteem,

00:52:58.508 --> 00:53:00.385
and build the clientele as well.

00:53:00.385 --> 00:53:02.971
- This is the magic of storage.

00:53:04.097 --> 00:53:06.600
What you think it will be inside?

00:53:06.600 --> 00:53:09.102
You will afraid to go farther.

00:53:13.523 --> 00:53:15.734
- [Sabina] The park
completely transformed.

00:53:15.734 --> 00:53:17.027
People gathered,

00:53:17.027 --> 00:53:20.113
they started looking from
their apartment buildings,

00:53:20.113 --> 00:53:23.450
and we could see the change in the park.

00:53:26.912 --> 00:53:29.039
- I'm making roti.

00:53:29.039 --> 00:53:31.958
Roti in Persian we say this is naan,

00:53:31.958 --> 00:53:34.169
and the dough for roti is different

00:53:34.169 --> 00:53:36.963
because we add yeast for this one.

00:53:36.963 --> 00:53:39.299
- [Sabina] The park serve
as a common space for them

00:53:39.299 --> 00:53:41.760
where they meet immigrants
and new Canadians

00:53:41.760 --> 00:53:45.472
and spend some good
time having tea, coffee.

00:53:45.472 --> 00:53:47.390
That was the main goal.

00:53:49.893 --> 00:53:53.021
- We are waiting, waiting
until Friday come.

00:53:53.021 --> 00:53:57.192
When we, Friday come, we
come out just we enjoy.

00:53:57.192 --> 00:53:58.860
There is something,

00:53:58.860 --> 00:54:01.780
just because of market we come out.

00:54:03.573 --> 00:54:05.492
- As in the new immigrants,

00:54:05.492 --> 00:54:08.912
a lot of women go through
stress and isolation on

00:54:08.912 --> 00:54:10.830
a regular basis.

00:54:10.830 --> 00:54:14.417
And we thought like
this market would serve

00:54:16.002 --> 00:54:18.255
as a stress relieving agent

00:54:19.547 --> 00:54:22.467
or something like that where people feel

00:54:22.467 --> 00:54:25.762
really good when they come outdoors.

00:54:25.762 --> 00:54:30.433
- I mean it's good to
empower empowerment in womens

00:54:30.433 --> 00:54:32.435
with support to get house,

00:54:32.435 --> 00:54:33.853
to get your lands, yeah.

00:54:33.853 --> 00:54:36.940
So, yeah there's the main goals

00:54:36.940 --> 00:54:39.693
to empowering the women, ladies.

00:54:40.944 --> 00:54:42.862
- Did you buy any tray for yourself?

00:54:42.862 --> 00:54:44.656
Did you buy any canopy for yourself?

00:54:44.656 --> 00:54:46.741
Next year I'll be (speaking
foreign language),

00:54:46.741 --> 00:54:48.994
you won't be coming to the market.

00:54:48.994 --> 00:54:50.328
There are challenges

00:54:50.328 --> 00:54:53.081
and there are achievements, right?

00:54:53.081 --> 00:54:57.127
We started with zero,
nothing in our hands.

00:54:57.127 --> 00:54:58.962
But we had a strong vision.

00:54:58.962 --> 00:55:02.090
We had that commitment to
give it to the community,

00:55:02.090 --> 00:55:05.218
to return back to the community.

00:55:05.218 --> 00:55:08.471
The community comes up
with their own ideas,

00:55:08.471 --> 00:55:10.432
and all because they feel comfortable.

00:55:10.432 --> 00:55:13.393
They feel, they see us as,

00:55:13.393 --> 00:55:15.895
the part of their own self

00:55:15.895 --> 00:55:18.732
which is really challenging for us to take

00:55:18.732 --> 00:55:22.068
everyone's suggestion
to work on one thing,

00:55:22.068 --> 00:55:26.364
but we do acts of the
suggestions, and we choose

00:55:26.364 --> 00:55:27.198
how best

00:55:28.158 --> 00:55:32.203
those things fit into what
we are really trying to do.

00:55:32.203 --> 00:55:35.040
I mean looking at the bigger goal.

00:55:37.667 --> 00:55:39.919
Her husband passed away recently,

00:55:39.919 --> 00:55:43.757
and for her these are
the opportunities which

00:55:43.757 --> 00:55:45.800
provide supplement income for her

00:55:45.800 --> 00:55:47.802
to run her house, and she has kids,

00:55:47.802 --> 00:55:49.429
and she has to look after them,

00:55:49.429 --> 00:55:53.016
and the more over is when
she comes to the market,

00:55:53.016 --> 00:55:55.060
she comes with the friends,

00:55:55.060 --> 00:55:56.895
and they are supportive,

00:55:56.895 --> 00:55:59.272
and you know they are
always there for her.

00:55:59.272 --> 00:56:01.775
It's like a building family

00:56:01.775 --> 00:56:03.902
kind of environment in the market,

00:56:03.902 --> 00:56:07.947
and they're all friends
come to the market to eat

00:56:07.947 --> 00:56:11.785
what she is making and they love doing it.

00:56:11.785 --> 00:56:13.286
- Thank you enjoy.

00:56:16.039 --> 00:56:18.541
- You know when I look at their faces,

00:56:18.541 --> 00:56:20.460
I can see in their eyes, you know.

00:56:20.460 --> 00:56:22.712
I can sense that happiness.

00:56:24.172 --> 00:56:28.760
That is, I think, that's the
value this market is bringing.

00:56:28.760 --> 00:56:30.178
It's emotionally.

00:56:31.846 --> 00:56:34.682
Though there is definitely a
financial support for them,

00:56:34.682 --> 00:56:37.060
but still it is like more about

00:56:37.060 --> 00:56:39.854
building relationship, building trust.

00:56:39.854 --> 00:56:41.064
- She is very nice

00:56:41.064 --> 00:56:45.402
and she always support
us and this she bring,

00:56:45.402 --> 00:56:48.863
thank you so, much.
- Aw, my pleasure.

00:56:48.863 --> 00:56:52.117
(chamber music)

00:56:52.117 --> 00:56:56.162
- Maybe there is no reward
when cooperation works well.

00:56:56.162 --> 00:56:58.957
Maybe you're just in the process,

00:56:58.957 --> 00:57:01.376
and the process is the reward.

00:57:01.376 --> 00:57:05.171
There's something that's
innately satisfying about that.

00:57:05.171 --> 00:57:07.841
(chamber music)

00:57:12.137 --> 00:57:13.930
- Sorry, sorry can we do it again?

00:57:13.930 --> 00:57:16.141
We didn't start together I don't know why.

00:57:16.141 --> 00:57:17.642
If we weren't able to work together,

00:57:17.642 --> 00:57:20.854
music itself would suffer,
and it would be, probably,

00:57:20.854 --> 00:57:24.149
we wouldn't be able to communicate
effectively as a group.

00:57:24.149 --> 00:57:26.609
The music might still sound like music,

00:57:26.609 --> 00:57:28.153
but it just wouldn't be convincing.

00:57:28.153 --> 00:57:30.280
We wouldn't have the organic quality

00:57:30.280 --> 00:57:32.615
that we are always striving for.

00:57:32.615 --> 00:57:35.285
(chamber music)

00:57:48.923 --> 00:57:50.717
Should we just do it one
more time with Sungyong now?

00:57:50.717 --> 00:57:53.636
And then I'll join in the
end 'cause my part's easy.

00:57:53.636 --> 00:57:55.972
Yeah let's see how it works.

00:57:58.224 --> 00:57:59.851
Here's our motor right
we have this engine,

00:57:59.851 --> 00:58:02.270
then we have the car driver.

00:58:02.270 --> 00:58:04.939
(chamber music)

00:58:13.698 --> 00:58:16.117
- The process itself is...

00:58:16.117 --> 00:58:19.412
You feel very satisfied, you
know you go through stages

00:58:19.412 --> 00:58:20.663
and you come to a different result,

00:58:20.663 --> 00:58:22.916
but all the stages, you know,

00:58:22.916 --> 00:58:24.417
it's enjoyable.

00:58:24.417 --> 00:58:25.793
- I would just say, it's a lot more fun.

00:58:25.793 --> 00:58:27.670
There's certain times
when you have to play

00:58:27.670 --> 00:58:29.964
like a soloist from the virtuosic passage

00:58:29.964 --> 00:58:31.090
in every one of our parts.

00:58:31.090 --> 00:58:34.928
And there's no leader, but
then we're all leaders, too.

00:58:34.928 --> 00:58:37.347
It's just a great experience.

00:58:37.347 --> 00:58:40.016
(chamber music)

00:58:45.897 --> 00:58:47.899
- [Lyle] I don't know if
I was a bad guy or not,

00:58:47.899 --> 00:58:50.485
but I definitely wasn't a good guy.

00:58:50.485 --> 00:58:53.488
You know you gotta leave
that shit behind you

00:58:53.488 --> 00:58:55.406
if you wanna grow right?

00:58:55.406 --> 00:58:59.327
And so, that's what
I've been trying to do.

00:58:59.327 --> 00:59:02.539
- The farms in themselves by
nature of what we're doing here

00:59:02.539 --> 00:59:04.874
are kind of like the great equalizer.

00:59:04.874 --> 00:59:08.461
If you're out there picking
radishes or filling boxes

00:59:08.461 --> 00:59:10.129
or planting trees or...

00:59:10.129 --> 00:59:12.006
It doesn't matter what your history is,

00:59:12.006 --> 00:59:13.758
how much suffering you've been through,

00:59:13.758 --> 00:59:16.135
it really levels you off.

00:59:16.135 --> 00:59:19.389
- Yeah it was a good opportunity to get

00:59:20.640 --> 00:59:23.810
into the giving back department a bit.

00:59:25.645 --> 00:59:28.022
Working for your community a bit, right,

00:59:28.022 --> 00:59:30.149
instead of taking from it all the time.

00:59:30.149 --> 00:59:33.444
- It wasn't a big idea that
we were gonna go out and

00:59:33.444 --> 00:59:34.612
rescue people.

00:59:36.322 --> 00:59:39.617
Our idea was set the table here,

00:59:39.617 --> 00:59:40.785
and do it with

00:59:41.703 --> 00:59:44.247
soil and seeds and growing food,

00:59:44.247 --> 00:59:45.415
and allow that

00:59:46.332 --> 00:59:47.417
process to do

00:59:48.501 --> 00:59:50.753
what it does for all of us.

00:59:52.171 --> 00:59:54.257
People ask me why I farm.

00:59:55.383 --> 00:59:56.926
It's as much for my mental health

00:59:56.926 --> 00:59:58.886
as it is for anybody
else who's working here.

00:59:58.886 --> 00:59:59.846
I'm in no different.

00:59:59.846 --> 01:00:01.848
We're different in some ways,

01:00:01.848 --> 01:00:03.349
but we're all alike in other ways,

01:00:03.349 --> 01:00:04.809
we all have our stuff.
- It is spiritual for me too.

01:00:04.809 --> 01:00:07.895
Like you put something in
the, a seed in the ground,

01:00:07.895 --> 01:00:09.063
ya nurture it,

01:00:09.897 --> 01:00:11.816
ya care for it,

01:00:11.816 --> 01:00:13.735
and it gives you something back.

01:00:13.735 --> 01:00:17.322
Yeah it is something
actually that changes,

01:00:18.615 --> 01:00:19.532
changes ya.

01:00:24.412 --> 01:00:26.581
- We've survived a lot of crazy stuff,

01:00:26.581 --> 01:00:28.207
but I think it's important enough

01:00:28.207 --> 01:00:30.251
to the people who we work with

01:00:30.251 --> 01:00:32.462
that it has to continue.

01:00:32.462 --> 01:00:35.965
And it's important enough that
it has to include more people

01:00:35.965 --> 01:00:38.885
who haven't been able to take part.

01:00:40.595 --> 01:00:42.930
Our goal is not, unlike most
social service agencies,

01:00:42.930 --> 01:00:45.975
is not to graduate people
and send them down the road.

01:00:45.975 --> 01:00:47.894
No, we feel like

01:00:47.894 --> 01:00:49.979
we want them to be

01:00:49.979 --> 01:00:53.274
for as long as they want
to be able to come here.

01:00:53.274 --> 01:00:54.817
- Yeah you guys are my family right?

01:00:54.817 --> 01:00:56.569
- The first time I met this guy,

01:00:56.569 --> 01:00:59.072
I was like who is this guy you know?

01:00:59.072 --> 01:01:01.407
And you had kind of a hard edge.

01:01:01.407 --> 01:01:02.950
- Well I wasn't very

01:01:02.950 --> 01:01:04.911
like socially integrated either.

01:01:04.911 --> 01:01:07.163
- Well you were new.
- Yeah, I was.

01:01:07.163 --> 01:01:10.416
- [Michael] This guy's
got the softest core.

01:01:10.416 --> 01:01:11.793
- You hear that guys?

01:01:11.793 --> 01:01:14.212
That better be on tape man.
- He has a good heart.

01:01:14.212 --> 01:01:15.213
He's got a good heart.

01:01:15.213 --> 01:01:17.048
- I might need that one
day as character evidence.

01:01:17.048 --> 01:01:19.592
- [Michael] That's right.

01:01:19.592 --> 01:01:22.637
No I mean it, it blows my mind,

01:01:22.637 --> 01:01:24.514
somebody who's gone
through the kind of shit

01:01:24.514 --> 01:01:26.474
that you've gone through and that you're--

01:01:26.474 --> 01:01:28.434
- Yeah I appreciate that Michael, I do.

01:01:28.434 --> 01:01:30.186
- You guys don't have
the monopoly on suffering

01:01:30.186 --> 01:01:31.688
by the way.
- Oh yes I know this.

01:01:31.688 --> 01:01:34.482
- And so, I'm just saying, for me,

01:01:36.526 --> 01:01:38.653
I needed to do this

01:01:38.653 --> 01:01:41.114
for myself as much as for anybody else,

01:01:41.114 --> 01:01:43.533
and that's the honest truth.

01:01:43.533 --> 01:01:44.951
It's hard to admit but it's true.

01:01:44.951 --> 01:01:47.286
I mean, I got my own
stuff I gotta deal with.

01:01:47.286 --> 01:01:49.956
(chamber music)

01:01:58.172 --> 01:02:00.925
- [Nikita] Actually, the
music is sort of like

01:02:00.925 --> 01:02:02.093
a reflection of life.

01:02:02.093 --> 01:02:05.596
This medium allows to have a conversation

01:02:05.596 --> 01:02:07.640
with a different

01:02:07.640 --> 01:02:10.184
people at different times,

01:02:10.184 --> 01:02:12.103
and if it's works well,

01:02:13.104 --> 01:02:17.066
it's really good. (laughs)

01:02:17.066 --> 01:02:19.736
(chamber music)

01:02:35.918 --> 01:02:40.006
- It's very clear to me that
to have a healthy system,

01:02:40.006 --> 01:02:43.426
finance is a piece of the bigger whole,

01:02:43.426 --> 01:02:46.095
and needs to be in service of that whole.

01:02:46.095 --> 01:02:49.348
The basic premise of investment

01:02:49.348 --> 01:02:53.728
is to invest money in order to
create more money at the end.

01:02:53.728 --> 01:02:55.897
We call that return on capital,

01:02:55.897 --> 01:02:59.400
but it's money in creating more money out.

01:03:01.235 --> 01:03:04.113
So, we've grown a pile of money

01:03:04.113 --> 01:03:06.199
while we've destroyed the atmosphere.

01:03:06.199 --> 01:03:08.201
That's not sustainable.

01:03:08.201 --> 01:03:10.161
- There's a whole sense is that

01:03:10.161 --> 01:03:12.580
we've gotta go beyond just profit making,

01:03:12.580 --> 01:03:16.626
which takes no regard for what
happens to the environment.

01:03:16.626 --> 01:03:20.171
And another piece of it is
human and communitarian,

01:03:20.171 --> 01:03:23.674
trying to build a different
sense of community.

01:03:23.674 --> 01:03:26.385
But those themes I think
the democracy, wealth,

01:03:26.385 --> 01:03:28.137
changing wealth and
ownership, environment,

01:03:28.137 --> 01:03:30.306
community, participation,

01:03:30.306 --> 01:03:32.350
those are the themes
that we're seeing develop

01:03:32.350 --> 01:03:36.479
in many parts of the country
and in many parts of the world.

01:03:36.479 --> 01:03:39.148
(chamber music)

01:03:44.320 --> 01:03:45.988
- I think of the first
time the beer was served

01:03:45.988 --> 01:03:46.906
at the Root Cellar.

01:03:46.906 --> 01:03:49.283
I mean that feeling is tremendous,

01:03:49.283 --> 01:03:52.119
and you realize that hey
people are enjoying this,

01:03:52.119 --> 01:03:53.371
it's not just us.

01:03:53.371 --> 01:03:56.958
There's a great mix of
younger folks, older folks,

01:03:56.958 --> 01:03:59.502
women, men, those who were coming in

01:03:59.502 --> 01:04:02.672
who might not feel that the beer

01:04:02.672 --> 01:04:04.841
culture is currently
really speaking to them

01:04:04.841 --> 01:04:06.342
that might feel alienated.

01:04:06.342 --> 01:04:08.970
I think our approach helps to

01:04:08.970 --> 01:04:11.889
kind of even that playing
field to where it's like

01:04:11.889 --> 01:04:13.516
it's a good beverage.

01:04:13.516 --> 01:04:15.351
- I love craft beer.

01:04:15.351 --> 01:04:16.185
I just do.

01:04:16.185 --> 01:04:19.105
And really in London
there's not a lot of options

01:04:19.105 --> 01:04:20.982
right now and as soon
as I found it I'm like

01:04:20.982 --> 01:04:23.693
I'm just happy that I
have a beer that I enjoy

01:04:23.693 --> 01:04:26.487
and the whole co-op idea is so cool.

01:04:26.487 --> 01:04:29.657
I've actually never experienced
that in a beer-wise.

01:04:29.657 --> 01:04:31.409
- The big breweries out there who are,

01:04:31.409 --> 01:04:33.327
they're sales and volume

01:04:33.327 --> 01:04:35.162
and that's their market.

01:04:35.162 --> 01:04:36.956
The more they can sell the better.

01:04:36.956 --> 01:04:38.916
- There's a lot of beers, you drink one

01:04:38.916 --> 01:04:41.919
and it tastes the same as
the other and these ones...

01:04:41.919 --> 01:04:44.046
When you look down even
the colors are different,

01:04:44.046 --> 01:04:45.381
the flavors are different,

01:04:45.381 --> 01:04:47.592
it's worth supporting, yeah.

01:04:47.592 --> 01:04:48.593
- When they name some of their beers,

01:04:48.593 --> 01:04:50.136
you recognize the name of

01:04:50.136 --> 01:04:51.929
it was the Spriggit Stout.

01:04:51.929 --> 01:04:54.140
These are farms that I grew
up around the corner from.

01:04:54.140 --> 01:04:55.892
They're bringing in local ingredients

01:04:55.892 --> 01:04:58.895
and making a fantastic beer with it.

01:05:00.229 --> 01:05:01.564
There's, there's morality
to their business.

01:05:01.564 --> 01:05:03.441
Like these guys aren't gonna make it rich,

01:05:03.441 --> 01:05:04.609
that's not the point of their business.

01:05:04.609 --> 01:05:07.153
They're providing a good
beer for the community,

01:05:07.153 --> 01:05:08.654
making jobs in the community.

01:05:08.654 --> 01:05:12.408
It's a fantastic way to kinda go about it.

01:05:12.408 --> 01:05:14.911
- We've known for decades

01:05:14.911 --> 01:05:17.705
that if you circulate money

01:05:17.705 --> 01:05:20.041
and materials in a local economy,

01:05:20.041 --> 01:05:22.793
you get these multiplier effects.

01:05:23.628 --> 01:05:26.339
By circulating money and
materials and information

01:05:26.339 --> 01:05:27.965
in a local economy,

01:05:27.965 --> 01:05:29.592
you're gonna be more in alignment

01:05:29.592 --> 01:05:32.470
with the regenerative
process that actually

01:05:32.470 --> 01:05:36.474
run, you know, it's the
way the world works.

01:05:36.474 --> 01:05:39.060
- What I really love about that
kind of community engagement

01:05:39.060 --> 01:05:40.436
is our neighborhood tart.

01:05:40.436 --> 01:05:42.438
So, it was a wheat base with rhubarb,

01:05:42.438 --> 01:05:44.941
and that rhubarb came
from old east village.

01:05:44.941 --> 01:05:46.400
So, we had somebody take up the cause,

01:05:46.400 --> 01:05:47.693
she went around

01:05:47.693 --> 01:05:48.986
and just chats on the street

01:05:48.986 --> 01:05:51.155
trying to find rhubarb
in people's backyards.

01:05:51.155 --> 01:05:52.323
We got a ton of it,

01:05:52.323 --> 01:05:53.574
we threw it into the brew,

01:05:53.574 --> 01:05:54.867
it was really tasty,

01:05:54.867 --> 01:05:57.036
and I think for us it again just...

01:05:57.036 --> 01:05:58.579
it's a lot of fun.

01:05:58.579 --> 01:06:01.958
I mean, beer can be a lot of fun.

01:06:01.958 --> 01:06:06.170
Right now we brew an
incredibly inefficient beer.

01:06:06.170 --> 01:06:08.714
It is not a turn key process.

01:06:08.714 --> 01:06:11.801
It is not pressing a few
buttons and things work.

01:06:11.801 --> 01:06:16.138
It is a labor of love, but it
is a lot of labor right now.

01:06:16.138 --> 01:06:18.975
And so, we're hoping to
streamline some of those processes

01:06:18.975 --> 01:06:21.894
by an expansion so, that we can focus on

01:06:21.894 --> 01:06:25.272
the kind of value that we feel
our beer is bringing as well.

01:06:25.272 --> 01:06:28.109
- The hops for the next
one are sitting over there

01:06:28.109 --> 01:06:31.737
with the Irish moss in the latest edition.

01:06:31.737 --> 01:06:34.073
- Brewing is, it's an
economy to scale thing.

01:06:34.073 --> 01:06:35.783
It takes largely the same amount of labor

01:06:35.783 --> 01:06:37.284
to produce one barrel of beer

01:06:37.284 --> 01:06:40.871
as it does to produce
10 times that amount.

01:06:40.871 --> 01:06:42.999
- The London Brewing
Co-op at its current size,

01:06:42.999 --> 01:06:45.376
it fiscally doesn't work.

01:06:45.376 --> 01:06:47.253
So, we need to scale up.

01:06:47.253 --> 01:06:50.214
And in order to scale
up we need more space,

01:06:50.214 --> 01:06:51.632
we need,

01:06:51.632 --> 01:06:55.136
well, resources, time, energy, to do that.

01:06:58.347 --> 01:06:59.974
We're gonna need some
investors, willing investors

01:06:59.974 --> 01:07:01.767
to believe in the co-op model,

01:07:01.767 --> 01:07:02.685
believe in our product,

01:07:02.685 --> 01:07:04.895
and believe in a local food movement.

01:07:04.895 --> 01:07:06.814
- I don't know that Josie's
ever been down here.

01:07:06.814 --> 01:07:09.942
- Okay.
- So, I think a lot--

01:07:09.942 --> 01:07:11.986
- [David] Is demonstrating what works?

01:07:11.986 --> 01:07:13.779
- What's here.

01:07:13.779 --> 01:07:17.867
- We know what the expansion
looks like, 225 to 250.

01:07:17.867 --> 01:07:20.536
That's not including a purchase.

01:07:21.662 --> 01:07:24.498
Have I got an investment
opportunity for you,

01:07:24.498 --> 01:07:28.127
and in the first year
you'll double your money.

01:07:28.127 --> 01:07:30.629
- Nice to see you.
- Yeah good to see you.

01:07:30.629 --> 01:07:31.964
Thanks for joining us.

01:07:31.964 --> 01:07:33.674
- Thanks for having us in your business.

01:07:33.674 --> 01:07:36.177
- If you're up for it, love to chat about

01:07:36.177 --> 01:07:37.928
what we've done here,

01:07:37.928 --> 01:07:40.264
and maybe where we could
go into the future.

01:07:40.264 --> 01:07:44.268
Financing in part may come
from Londoners who support

01:07:44.268 --> 01:07:45.895
our vision and our dream.

01:07:45.895 --> 01:07:49.482
And we want it to be angel
or patient investment

01:07:49.482 --> 01:07:53.110
where they believe in the
purpose of what we're doing,

01:07:53.110 --> 01:07:56.197
not just the fiscal return
that they will receive.

01:07:56.197 --> 01:07:58.657
- I think certainly those
short-term goals that

01:07:58.657 --> 01:08:00.993
the expansion of the brewery system

01:08:00.993 --> 01:08:03.871
that would be in a lease situation.

01:08:03.871 --> 01:08:06.499
- So, it's specific investors
that we're looking for who

01:08:06.499 --> 01:08:09.627
understand our vision and believe in it.

01:08:09.627 --> 01:08:10.669
- And that'll include

01:08:10.669 --> 01:08:13.047
doing the renovations
in the next building,

01:08:13.047 --> 01:08:14.799
moving the brewery there

01:08:14.799 --> 01:08:16.717
and having it up and going?

01:08:16.717 --> 01:08:17.551
Okay.

01:08:17.551 --> 01:08:18.886
- [David] Yeah we'll get
that to you certainly.

01:08:18.886 --> 01:08:20.721
- [Josie] Yeah absolutely,
and if you have any questions,

01:08:20.721 --> 01:08:23.349
anything putting into it just let me know.

01:08:23.349 --> 01:08:25.768
Yeah absolutely.
- Fantastic yeah.

01:08:25.768 --> 01:08:28.354
- It drives me nuts when
I start talking this way

01:08:28.354 --> 01:08:30.523
and people ah you're a
socialist or you're a communist.

01:08:30.523 --> 01:08:31.816
This has nothing to do with,

01:08:31.816 --> 01:08:35.903
this has nothing to do with
socialism versus capitalism.

01:08:35.903 --> 01:08:39.573
This is how real systems
in the real world work,

01:08:39.573 --> 01:08:43.077
and that's physics and biology and energy.

01:08:44.161 --> 01:08:45.788
Everything is energy.

01:08:45.788 --> 01:08:47.540
This is the way it works from a tornado,

01:08:47.540 --> 01:08:49.708
to boiling water in a pot,

01:08:51.961 --> 01:08:54.630
to insects, to entire ecosystems,

01:08:54.630 --> 01:08:56.924
to human bodies, to human consciousness,

01:08:56.924 --> 01:09:01.053
to the entire universe itself
is a learning, living system.

01:09:01.053 --> 01:09:03.681
(mellow music)

01:09:08.394 --> 01:09:12.690
- We are in Whistler, BC at
the Impact Economy Summit.

01:09:12.690 --> 01:09:14.066
We've been trying to cook

01:09:14.066 --> 01:09:17.695
a new economic model for
the citizen media ecosystem.

01:09:17.695 --> 01:09:20.114
There's a lot of energy in
the room with ups and downs,

01:09:20.114 --> 01:09:21.407
which is normal for this kind of

01:09:21.407 --> 01:09:25.202
interactions, discussions,
and working together.

01:09:25.202 --> 01:09:27.913
So, this is building bridges, right.

01:09:27.913 --> 01:09:30.624
Building bridges between the open world

01:09:30.624 --> 01:09:32.042
and the classical world

01:09:32.042 --> 01:09:35.421
so, that you, we can establish
these flows of resources

01:09:35.421 --> 01:09:37.548
in this process of metamorphosis.

01:09:37.548 --> 01:09:41.093
We can't just start building
from scratch from nothing.

01:09:41.093 --> 01:09:42.720
We are within the system,

01:09:42.720 --> 01:09:45.764
and by establishing these
interfaces and the trust,

01:09:45.764 --> 01:09:48.392
we can allow these resources to flow.

01:09:48.392 --> 01:09:52.062
- This is not in any way a
classical business right?

01:09:52.062 --> 01:09:54.064
This is not around a corporation

01:09:54.064 --> 01:09:56.525
that wants to maximize
profit for its shareholders.

01:09:56.525 --> 01:10:00.487
- I see traditional corporations as boxes.

01:10:00.487 --> 01:10:02.990
So, there are spaces of confinement

01:10:02.990 --> 01:10:05.618
that limits you in what you wanna do.

01:10:05.618 --> 01:10:07.745
Let's say you have a project
and you go to a corporation,

01:10:07.745 --> 01:10:09.914
and the question is do we have the budget,

01:10:09.914 --> 01:10:11.707
or the budget, that number is a

01:10:11.707 --> 01:10:13.834
parameter that confines you.

01:10:13.834 --> 01:10:15.878
Do we have the skills?

01:10:15.878 --> 01:10:17.463
So, essentially you're looking in a box

01:10:17.463 --> 01:10:18.631
and you see what's in there?

01:10:18.631 --> 01:10:20.674
How much money we have,
how much people we have.

01:10:20.674 --> 01:10:23.135
So, NASA is doing a lot of prizes

01:10:23.135 --> 01:10:25.971
and this is competitive crowd sourcing.

01:10:25.971 --> 01:10:27.723
You see you have a prize, you
have a project, and you say,

01:10:27.723 --> 01:10:30.309
alright who comes up with the best design,

01:10:30.309 --> 01:10:31.810
they get the prize.

01:10:31.810 --> 01:10:33.437
And there's a lot of redundancy,

01:10:33.437 --> 01:10:36.774
and everyone spends money
for their own prototyping,

01:10:36.774 --> 01:10:38.525
and for delivering something,

01:10:38.525 --> 01:10:41.070
and then you have one winner
and the rest are losers,

01:10:41.070 --> 01:10:44.740
and the only thing they take
home is the experience, right?

01:10:44.740 --> 01:10:47.451
So, with the value
accounting system, we say,

01:10:47.451 --> 01:10:49.578
well why don't you collaborate,

01:10:49.578 --> 01:10:51.330
and log your contributions,

01:10:51.330 --> 01:10:54.208
financial materials, time,
everything you put in there,

01:10:54.208 --> 01:10:56.168
and then the prize you share it

01:10:56.168 --> 01:10:58.170
according to everyone's contribution.

01:10:58.170 --> 01:11:00.506
Okay, so you turn
competitive crowd sourcing

01:11:00.506 --> 01:11:02.925
into collaborative crowd sourcing.

01:11:02.925 --> 01:11:05.594
- What you're building
is a set of businesses

01:11:05.594 --> 01:11:07.429
that feed the community.

01:11:07.429 --> 01:11:10.391
'Cause this is a real paradigm
shift what you're doing.

01:11:10.391 --> 01:11:12.226
This is not capitalism as we know it,

01:11:12.226 --> 01:11:13.644
this is something entirely different.

01:11:13.644 --> 01:11:14.728
- [Tibi] Yes.

01:11:15.646 --> 01:11:18.190
- Then it's clear that this is really

01:11:18.190 --> 01:11:19.441
another way to go for me.

01:11:19.441 --> 01:11:21.068
This is the kind of answer

01:11:21.068 --> 01:11:24.154
to a big problem that
we have today, which is

01:11:24.154 --> 01:11:26.407
you don't get paid for
things you like to do,

01:11:26.407 --> 01:11:28.951
you only get paid for the
things you don't like to do.

01:11:28.951 --> 01:11:32.955
So, I think the system
that is actually able

01:11:32.955 --> 01:11:36.250
to create livelihoods
without destroying the planet

01:11:36.250 --> 01:11:38.419
and without creating social strife

01:11:38.419 --> 01:11:41.463
is the one that eventually
hopefully has to win out,

01:11:41.463 --> 01:11:44.633
because otherwise we're all doomed anyway.

01:11:44.633 --> 01:11:46.218
- [Woman] If everyone
wants to take their seats,

01:11:46.218 --> 01:11:47.970
that would be lovely.

01:11:49.972 --> 01:11:53.434
- I will go home with a positive message,

01:11:53.434 --> 01:11:56.061
that what we're doing is important,

01:11:56.061 --> 01:11:58.522
that it's perceived as important,

01:11:58.522 --> 01:12:01.525
which is a validation of what we do.

01:12:07.614 --> 01:12:10.284
That's why I live for
these kinds of moments.

01:12:10.284 --> 01:12:13.370
This is the currency I'm looking for.

01:12:17.499 --> 01:12:20.502
I met some people that said
you Tibi, you can't fail,

01:12:20.502 --> 01:12:24.298
because you've been there
and you've paved the way,

01:12:24.298 --> 01:12:26.425
and if Sensorica fails,

01:12:26.425 --> 01:12:29.303
then it sends this message.

01:12:29.303 --> 01:12:31.221
Then I said, holy shit,
so, now I'm kind of

01:12:31.221 --> 01:12:33.098
trapped with all this responsibility

01:12:33.098 --> 01:12:35.768
to show that yes it can be done.

01:12:36.935 --> 01:12:38.979
Everything is converging towards

01:12:38.979 --> 01:12:40.856
a more collaborative economy for sure.

01:12:40.856 --> 01:12:44.985
And when you free people
economically, they're free.

01:12:44.985 --> 01:12:47.571
(upbeat music)

01:12:51.283 --> 01:12:53.243
(tape rips)

01:12:53.243 --> 01:12:55.162
I like that noise.

01:12:55.162 --> 01:12:56.497
The tape,

01:12:56.497 --> 01:12:58.499
it's like you're moving man.

01:12:58.499 --> 01:13:02.628
The actual lab space that we
have now is pretty packed.

01:13:02.628 --> 01:13:04.338
There's a lot of people

01:13:04.338 --> 01:13:06.382
so, very crowded.

01:13:06.382 --> 01:13:09.009
It's kind of tough on us to have to move,

01:13:09.009 --> 01:13:10.511
but it's something that we have to do,

01:13:10.511 --> 01:13:11.553
and we're pretty tired.

01:13:11.553 --> 01:13:13.305
We're pretty drained.

01:13:14.348 --> 01:13:17.351
There's a new chapter for Sensorica.

01:13:19.436 --> 01:13:21.814
We are really coming out.

01:13:21.814 --> 01:13:25.150
We're getting exposed downtown Montreal.

01:13:27.069 --> 01:13:29.571
I see myself as part of the Montreal

01:13:29.571 --> 01:13:31.156
new economy set up.

01:13:32.074 --> 01:13:33.992
We're taking over guys.

01:13:36.537 --> 01:13:38.080
The belly of the beast.

01:13:38.080 --> 01:13:42.251
Visibility and presence,
legitimacy, right in people's face.

01:13:43.919 --> 01:13:45.462
When it's time for you to go,

01:13:45.462 --> 01:13:48.173
you look back and you're
not gonna count your money

01:13:48.173 --> 01:13:50.134
or your material possessions.

01:13:50.134 --> 01:13:51.802
I think the things that will give you

01:13:51.802 --> 01:13:55.139
more satisfaction is do I leave a trace?

01:13:58.100 --> 01:14:02.855
Well the greatest hope for the
collaborative economy is to

01:14:02.855 --> 01:14:03.689
have this

01:14:04.898 --> 01:14:08.318
smooth transition without too many bumps,

01:14:09.361 --> 01:14:12.865
because we can feel that we're frustrating

01:14:12.865 --> 01:14:14.366
the losers,

01:14:14.366 --> 01:14:15.701
and they're powerful.

01:14:15.701 --> 01:14:18.537
(dramatic music)

01:14:35.387 --> 01:14:37.139
- There's no land.

01:14:37.139 --> 01:14:39.016
I'm hoping that we're gonna get

01:14:39.016 --> 01:14:40.684
some acreage in the middle of a park

01:14:40.684 --> 01:14:43.187
that has to be developed here.

01:14:44.563 --> 01:14:46.648
We need a permanent location.

01:14:46.648 --> 01:14:48.150
We need multiple locations,

01:14:48.150 --> 01:14:49.860
but we need one permanent location

01:14:49.860 --> 01:14:53.822
to replace Pacific when
the via ducts come down.

01:14:55.449 --> 01:14:56.700
We need

01:14:56.700 --> 01:14:59.077
more people who can

01:14:59.077 --> 01:15:01.246
really carry the workload.

01:15:03.207 --> 01:15:08.045
I think eventually we could,
if people felt that incentive,

01:15:08.045 --> 01:15:12.049
that they had something at
stake beyond their hourly wage,

01:15:12.049 --> 01:15:13.967
develop something that was,

01:15:13.967 --> 01:15:18.138
did return something to our
staff, but we're not there yet.

01:15:18.138 --> 01:15:19.348
I want them to come to work

01:15:19.348 --> 01:15:21.433
because they're working
for themselves, you know.

01:15:21.433 --> 01:15:22.768
They're working for their own healing,

01:15:22.768 --> 01:15:25.187
and for a community of farmers and eaters

01:15:25.187 --> 01:15:27.648
that surround it, you know?

01:15:27.648 --> 01:15:31.276
I want them to feel
that sense of ownership.

01:15:31.276 --> 01:15:33.946
(mellow music)

01:15:37.282 --> 01:15:40.244
I think it's probably
better than stock options or

01:15:40.244 --> 01:15:42.663
shared ownership or anything.

01:15:42.663 --> 01:15:44.540
I mean we've had this
idea of a cooperative,

01:15:44.540 --> 01:15:47.960
turning this into a
workers' owned cooperative.

01:15:47.960 --> 01:15:49.878
But the truth is what
would they be owning?

01:15:49.878 --> 01:15:51.880
There's nothing to own.

01:15:51.880 --> 01:15:53.173
What we can offer

01:15:53.173 --> 01:15:56.009
is this shared experience of what happens

01:15:56.009 --> 01:15:59.137
in the process of doing this work.

01:15:59.137 --> 01:16:01.431
- Okay. Oh awesome.

01:16:01.431 --> 01:16:02.891
I wanna retire here.

01:16:02.891 --> 01:16:05.686
I'm 56, I'll be 57 November,

01:16:05.686 --> 01:16:07.521
and this is it for me.

01:16:07.521 --> 01:16:09.648
I think it's my last
stop, if we last that long

01:16:09.648 --> 01:16:12.276
this is my last stop and I'm hoping we do.

01:16:12.276 --> 01:16:13.485
All we need is boxes.

01:16:13.485 --> 01:16:14.444
We can fill the boxes,

01:16:14.444 --> 01:16:16.947
we will put out whatever
we need the boxes.

01:16:16.947 --> 01:16:18.574
We need infrastructure,

01:16:18.574 --> 01:16:20.033
if we get the infrastructure,

01:16:20.033 --> 01:16:22.786
we will fill them up and we'll...

01:16:24.663 --> 01:16:27.249
We're good, we really are good.

01:16:28.875 --> 01:16:30.419
- Here's what the garden kinda

01:16:30.419 --> 01:16:33.213
taught me, if you're
not gonna help yourself,

01:16:33.213 --> 01:16:37.175
it's pretty hard for
other people to help ya.

01:16:37.175 --> 01:16:38.677
They can't.

01:16:38.677 --> 01:16:40.387
Like, you know what I mean?

01:16:40.387 --> 01:16:42.889
If you're not willing to do it,

01:16:42.889 --> 01:16:44.975
why should they invest all the time

01:16:44.975 --> 01:16:48.604
and fucking spinning their wheels?

01:16:48.604 --> 01:16:50.731
Man, if I didn't have this job,

01:16:50.731 --> 01:16:52.816
where would I be in the last 3 years?

01:16:52.816 --> 01:16:54.651
Like think about it, right.

01:16:54.651 --> 01:16:55.485
Dead,

01:16:57.154 --> 01:16:59.156
in an alley shooting up,

01:16:59.156 --> 01:17:00.907
or in a penitentiary.

01:17:00.907 --> 01:17:03.160
Those are my three options.

01:17:05.412 --> 01:17:07.331
Sole Food saved my life, right?

01:17:07.331 --> 01:17:09.666
What else can I say, it did.

01:17:11.293 --> 01:17:13.128
Over the carrot or over the leaves?

01:17:13.128 --> 01:17:14.755
- Over the carrots on the first one,

01:17:14.755 --> 01:17:16.548
then over the leaves on the rest.

01:17:16.548 --> 01:17:19.176
- For me at this stage in my life,

01:17:19.176 --> 01:17:20.927
this is value to me.

01:17:20.927 --> 01:17:23.722
Spending time here with my friends,

01:17:23.722 --> 01:17:26.183
feeling good about myself.

01:17:26.183 --> 01:17:27.893
Being able to close my eyes at night,

01:17:27.893 --> 01:17:31.605
saying I didn't hurt anybody today.

01:17:31.605 --> 01:17:34.232
I did maybe a couple good things.

01:17:34.232 --> 01:17:36.026
That's value to me.

01:17:36.026 --> 01:17:38.695
(mellow music)

01:17:41.740 --> 01:17:43.533
- So, what is real wealth?

01:17:43.533 --> 01:17:45.035
Well let me tell you what it's not.

01:17:45.035 --> 01:17:46.286
It's not money,

01:17:48.246 --> 01:17:51.458
but you know there are
people going around saying

01:17:51.458 --> 01:17:53.418
we need a new theory of value.

01:17:53.418 --> 01:17:55.629
So, we don't really know what that is.

01:17:55.629 --> 01:17:57.673
I can rattle off a few ideas.

01:17:57.673 --> 01:17:59.257
So, obviously

01:17:59.257 --> 01:18:01.677
fresh air to breathe,

01:18:01.677 --> 01:18:03.428
clean water to drink,

01:18:03.428 --> 01:18:05.180
access to clean food,

01:18:06.056 --> 01:18:09.434
family, friendships, community,

01:18:09.434 --> 01:18:13.605
art, beauty is obviously
what we know is real wealth.

01:18:16.024 --> 01:18:18.110
- I think that's a big thing, too,

01:18:18.110 --> 01:18:19.653
with the cooperative is that

01:18:19.653 --> 01:18:21.363
the cooperative shifts with the needs

01:18:21.363 --> 01:18:22.989
of its workers,

01:18:22.989 --> 01:18:26.159
not just with the needs
of the end product.

01:18:26.159 --> 01:18:28.829
(mellow music)

01:18:32.249 --> 01:18:33.417
I think the five of us

01:18:33.417 --> 01:18:35.752
I imagine soon won't be the five of us

01:18:35.752 --> 01:18:38.088
from a you know worker owner perspective.

01:18:38.088 --> 01:18:39.798
I think really we wanna see

01:18:39.798 --> 01:18:41.842
that full realization of the cooperative,

01:18:41.842 --> 01:18:45.303
so, that we can bring in new workers who

01:18:45.303 --> 01:18:47.347
have the same values that we do

01:18:47.347 --> 01:18:48.890
so, that when people come into work,

01:18:48.890 --> 01:18:50.517
it's 'cause they wanna be there.

01:18:50.517 --> 01:18:52.060
It's 'cause they know they're part of

01:18:52.060 --> 01:18:54.146
kind of a larger movement,

01:18:54.146 --> 01:18:55.147
and it's not

01:18:56.148 --> 01:18:58.900
to the detriment of who they are.

01:19:03.947 --> 01:19:05.657
- Of course, the timing is

01:19:05.657 --> 01:19:08.493
hilarious (laughs),

01:19:08.493 --> 01:19:09.453
'cause I don't know,

01:19:09.453 --> 01:19:11.329
is it legal to have
babies brew beer for us?

01:19:11.329 --> 01:19:12.372
I don't think so.

01:19:12.372 --> 01:19:16.918
- No, but babies with craft
beer marketing is really cute.

01:19:16.918 --> 01:19:19.504
(mellow music)

01:19:30.432 --> 01:19:33.101
(chamber music)

01:19:48.617 --> 01:19:51.161
- We'll never make really good progress

01:19:51.161 --> 01:19:53.330
until we realize that

01:19:53.330 --> 01:19:55.332
it's not a chore to get there.

01:19:55.332 --> 01:19:57.000
You know you don't have to

01:19:57.000 --> 01:20:00.420
have a really unenjoyable
life in service to this cause.

01:20:00.420 --> 01:20:02.214
It's gotta be fun right?

01:20:02.214 --> 01:20:05.217
I mean, fundamentally it
actually needs to be enjoyable.

01:20:05.217 --> 01:20:07.052
Just taking that approach

01:20:07.052 --> 01:20:08.720
to thinking about how we do activism,

01:20:08.720 --> 01:20:10.013
how we do social change,

01:20:10.013 --> 01:20:12.390
how we organize our work,

01:20:12.390 --> 01:20:14.226
I think it's one of the most powerful

01:20:14.226 --> 01:20:16.228
levers of social change.

01:20:19.481 --> 01:20:22.108
- I don't think we're gonna
wake up one day and say,

01:20:22.108 --> 01:20:24.402
we're now in a regenerative economy.

01:20:24.402 --> 01:20:26.863
How did we know we were
in the enlightenment?

01:20:26.863 --> 01:20:29.366
This is a lifetimes transition

01:20:30.325 --> 01:20:33.411
and the challenge is are
we gonna screw this up

01:20:33.411 --> 01:20:36.331
before we make the transition?

01:20:36.331 --> 01:20:38.416
The symptoms that we feel

01:20:38.416 --> 01:20:40.210
will lighten.

01:20:40.210 --> 01:20:41.670
It will feel different.

01:20:41.670 --> 01:20:44.172
It will feel healthy.

01:20:44.172 --> 01:20:47.592
And then we'll say wow, it feels better.

01:20:51.638 --> 01:20:53.265
- It's a very moving period of history,

01:20:53.265 --> 01:20:55.725
potentially big things can happen.

01:20:55.725 --> 01:20:58.728
I think we are at a moment maybe,

01:20:58.728 --> 01:21:02.399
always a maybe, that we
are laying the groundwork

01:21:02.399 --> 01:21:04.025
for potentially,

01:21:04.025 --> 01:21:07.195
not only transformation but
irreversible transformation.

01:21:07.195 --> 01:21:09.197
That's a big deal in history.

01:21:09.197 --> 01:21:11.366
A very interesting time.

01:21:11.366 --> 01:21:14.077
(chamber music)

